# ── R5 (SESSION_REGISTRY_V2 §4.3.1): EVERY external fork in the sweep is bounded ────────────────
# Measured 2026-07-29: `grep -c 'timeout ' bin/cc-reaper` = 0 — the sweep synchronously invoked three
# foreign binaries with no bound at all, one of them (cc-inbox-guard) the exact binary whose unbounded
# fork caused the 5-day gate blockage. Consequence: sweep p90 403s / p99 2,099s / max 55,659s (15.5h)
# against a 300s StartInterval, and a decision acted on minutes after the evidence it rests on.
#
# PATH alone cannot find timeout(1): this process runs under launchd with a minimal PATH that does not
# include Homebrew, which is exactly where coreutils installs it — so an interactive shell looks fine
# while the AUTOMATED caller (the only one that matters here) stays unbounded. Absolute-path fallback
# list required. Same resolution as cc-inbox-guard:109-118 and it2-wrapper's _resolve_timeout.
#
# SIZING — the bound must fit the BAND, not a bench. com.chrisren.cc-reaper.plist sets
# `ProcessType Background` + `Nice 5`, the Darwin background QoS band, where wall time is taxed
# several-fold versus a foreground measurement. A bound sized from a foreground run is a permanent
# non-verdict: it fires on healthy work and proves nothing. These are sized as WEDGE detectors — far
# above any healthy run, far below the 300s interval — not as latency targets.
#
# 2026-08-25 (recycle #219) — THE RULE ABOVE WAS RIGHT AND THE CONSTANTS NEVER OBEYED IT, because
# the rule was written and the numbers were never re-derived against a background-band run. Measured
# over this log's 1,646 completed sweeps (12 days): reconcile fired 8.7%, backlog-reap 42.9%,
# classify 68.7% — all three tracking load, i.e. behaving like detectors — and inbox-guard 95.0%,
# flat at 82-100% on every single day regardless of load. The A/B that settles it is recorded at the
# ride-along block near the end of this file: the same work costs 38s foreground and 249s under
# `taskpolicy -c background`, a 6.6x band tax, against a 60s bound. GUARD raised 60 -> 600 there.
#
# 2026-08-25 (recycle #223) — THE BACKLOG-REAP ACQUITTAL IN THE PARAGRAPH ABOVE DOES NOT HOLD, and
# the defect is in the INSTRUMENT'S RESOLUTION rather than in its data. inbox-guard was convicted on
# a PER-DAY series ("82-100% on every single day"); its three siblings were acquitted on a 12-day
# AGGREGATE. Re-derived per day, backlog-reap is not tracking load at all — it climbs monotonically
# 6.7% (08-15) to 100.0% (08-25, 76 fires and ZERO completions) and saturates. 42.9% is the mean of
# that curve. A discriminator applied at one resolution to the convicted and a coarser one to the
# acquitted cannot separate them, and the coarser reading is the flattering one.
# BACKLOG raised 60 -> 900 at its own block, on its own two-arm band A/B (96s foreground vs 617s
# under taskpolicy -c background, 6.4x — the 6.6x above, reproduced on a second subject).
# The rule this file states was right BOTH times; only one constant was re-derived the first time.
#
# 2026-08-25 (recycle #224) — THE CLASSIFY ACQUITTAL IN THE #219 PARAGRAPH DOES HOLD. Re-screened
# with the same instrument that overturned backlog-reap, because clause 9 of that finding says an
# exoneration rots exactly like a claim — but it rots, it does not automatically expire, and this one
# had not. Two corrections to HOW it was computed, neither of which changes the verdict:
#   (a) 68.7% MERGES TWO ARMS THAT MEAN OPPOSITE THINGS. Of 1,144 `bound-fired classify` lines over
#       1,679 sweeps, 853 are arm 1 (the 90s bound, after which the 3x retry runs and RESCUES 65.9%
#       of them) and 291 are arm 2 (the retry also fired: no verdict at all). Arm 1 firing is the
#       design working; arm 2 is a total loss. A census that greps `bound-fired classify` cannot
#       distinguish them — anchor on `: exceeded` vs `: retry exceeded`.
#   (b) PER DAY, arm 2 oscillates 1.8%-49.5% with 4 direction changes across 13 days, against
#       backlog-reap's monotone 6.7% -> 100.0% climb. It is a load tracker, not a ratchet.
# AND THE BOUND IS SIZED RIGHT, which is the part that had never been measured. Same two-arm band
# A/B as the ride-along block, on `cc-classify --all --json` (read-only; its one write path is gated
# on the tests-only CC_CLASSIFY_INTERACTIVE_HOLD_DISABLE), both arms returning an identical 7
# entries, run at load ~10:
#     foreground                  PRI 31 (19/19 samples across two runs)      5s
#     taskpolicy -c background    PRI  4 (60/60 samples)                     31s
# A 6.2x band tax — the third independent reproduction of this box's ratio (6.6x inbox-guard, 6.4x
# backlog-reap). 90s is 2.9x healthy IN-BAND cost, so unlike its two siblings this constant was
# never a foreground number: a classify that passes 90s is 3x healthy in-band and one that passes
# 270s is 8.7x, which is a genuine wedge either way. DO NOT WIDEN IT. The 291 arm-2 sweeps are the
# box wedging, faithfully detected — the remaining half of backlog row ee743fad3674, measured and
# found sound rather than fixed.
#
# AND THE SECOND CLAUSE ("far below the 300s interval") NO LONGER HOLDS AS WRITTEN, for a reason
# that postdates it: the single-instance sweep lock (below) SKIPs a tick that finds a live sweep
# rather than queueing it, which is what made an overrunning sweep safe. 46.2% of sweeps already
# exceed 300s and 6.7% exceed 600s, by design tolerance, not by fault. Classify's 3x retry alone
# admits a 90+270=360s worst case above the interval. Read the clause as: bound every fork so a
# WEDGE is always cut, and pay skipped ticks for it — not as a per-fork budget that must sum under
# 300s, which no longer has a solution on this box.
#
# No timeout(1) anywhere ⇒ run unbounded rather than break the call (degrade, never regress).
# Seams: CC_REAPER_TIMEOUT_BIN (set-but-EMPTY disables bounding) · CC_REAPER_{RECONCILE,BACKLOG,GUARD,CLASSIFY}_TIMEOUT_S
if [ -n "${CC_REAPER_TIMEOUT_BIN+set}" ]; then
  _RP_TIMEOUT_BIN="$CC_REAPER_TIMEOUT_BIN"
else
  _RP_TIMEOUT_BIN=""
  for _c in "$(command -v timeout 2>/dev/null || true)" "$(command -v gtimeout 2>/dev/null || true)" \
            /opt/homebrew/bin/timeout /usr/local/bin/timeout \
            /opt/homebrew/bin/gtimeout /usr/local/bin/gtimeout; do
    [ -n "$_c" ] && [ -x "$_c" ] && { _RP_TIMEOUT_BIN="$_c"; break; }
  done
fi
# rc 124 is timeout(1)'s "I fired" code and MUST stay distinguishable from the child's own failure —
# a bound that fires silently is indistinguishable from a binary that legitimately returned nothing,
# which is how an inert mechanism hides. Callers log 124 explicitly.
_rp_bounded() { # <secs> <cmd...> — run bounded; rc 124 = the bound fired
  local s="${1:-60}"; shift
  if [ -z "$_RP_TIMEOUT_BIN" ] || [ ! -x "$_RP_TIMEOUT_BIN" ]; then "$@"; return $?; fi
  "$_RP_TIMEOUT_BIN" -k 5 "$s" "$@"
}

# ── GARBAGE ARM (2026-08-07, the load-781 incident). The meltdown was not sessions — it was the
# residue of DEAD ones, which no sweep owned: 3,599 processes of which 1,146 were `ps` (959
# orphaned to launchd, ~288% CPU in aggregate), 299 orphaned interactive zsh, 43 cc-close-attrib
# wrappers outliving their dead binary (their >(tee) never EOFs — the session's own orphans hold
# the fd2 write-end), and lead-crash-watchdog daemons polling for leads that died hours earlier.
# Each orphan `ps` walk slows every other, so the pile is self-amplifying — and it starves THIS
# sweep's own bounds (reconcile 60s, classify 90s), which is why the reaper read "NO candidates
# (fail-closed)" at precisely the moment reaping mattered most. The arm therefore runs FIRST and
# is deliberately cheap: two /bin/ps forks + one awk, then explicit per-pid kills.
#
# KILL DISCIPLINE (hard rules): explicit pids from ONE snapshot · per-pid ucomm re-verification at
# kill time (a recycled pid whose comm changed is skipped) · TERM then KILL · NEVER a pattern-kill
# · NEVER a claude binary, kitty, iTerm2, tmux, or any pid whose SUBTREE holds a live claude ·
# NEVER a whitelisted daemon. Fail-open everywhere: any error skips that pid and the sweep goes on.
# DRY-RUN (no --reap) prints candidates and kills nothing. Kill switch: CC_REAPER_GARBAGE=0.
# Seams (tests): CC_REAPER_GARBAGE_PS_A/_PS_B fixture files replace the live snapshots;
# CC_REAPER_GARBAGE_KILL replaces kill(1) with a collector; CC_REAPER_WATCHDOG_DIR relocates the
# watchdog pid-file store.
#
# ── NEVER AN ANCESTOR OF THIS SWEEP (2026-08-15, backlog 8efd655b0fe1). The kill discipline above
# enumerates what must not be touched by IDENTITY — claude, kitty, iTerm2, a whitelisted daemon —
# and every clause is about somebody else's process. It has no clause about OURS, and the classifier
# selects on SHAPE: `orphan-bash` is any launchd-parented bash older than 600s whose args miss the
# whitelist. A GitHub Actions macOS runner starts its service as exactly that — `/bin/bash
# .../runsvc.sh`, reparented to launchd — so a sweep running inside a CI job classifies the process
# tree it is running in as garbage and TERMs it. MEASURED, 6 of 6 cut shards in the hermetic
# workflow (runs 31879973156 / 31882316087 / 31885942191 / 31890394871 / 31896109392 / 31898668582):
# `##[error]The runner has received a shutdown signal` arrives 0.4-0.7s after
# `>>> RUNNING tests/reap-sweep-bounds.bats`, destroying ~43 suites of evidence each time; and the
# runner-shaped fixture below reproduces the classification deterministically off-CI.
#
# The guard is an INVARIANT, not a platform exception: a process this sweep is descended from is by
# construction alive and holding the sweep, so it can never be the residue of a dead session that
# this arm exists to collect. It is placed at the ACTUATOR rather than in the classifier so that it
# covers all three call sites at once — the TERM pass, the KILL escalation, and the watchdog branch
# whose candidates never pass through the awk — and BEFORE the collector seam so a test can observe
# the refusal. Cost: one /bin/ps per ancestor, computed once per sweep (depth 3 from the launchd
# daemon on the operator's box), against an arm the header calls deliberately cheap.
_GS_ANCESTORS=""
_gs_ancestors_init() { # cache the pid chain of THIS process, self included. Live ps, never the
  # snapshot: under a fixtured snapshot the sweep's own pid is absent by construction, so a
  # snapshot-derived chain would be empty in exactly the tests that must exercise this guard.
  local p="$$" n=0
  _GS_ANCESTORS=""
  while [ -n "$p" ] && [ "$p" -gt 1 ] 2>/dev/null && [ "$n" -lt 64 ]; do
    _GS_ANCESTORS="$_GS_ANCESTORS,$p"
    p="$(/bin/ps -p "$p" -o ppid= 2>/dev/null | tr -d ' ')"
    n=$((n+1))
  done
  _GS_ANCESTORS="${_GS_ANCESTORS#,}"
}
_gs_is_ancestor() { # <pid> — 0 iff pid is this sweep or one of its ancestors
  case ",${_GS_ANCESTORS}," in *",${1},"*) return 0 ;; esac
  return 1
}
# ── THE COMM RE-CHECK IS VACUOUS FOR THE CLASS IT MATTERS MOST FOR (2026-08-16, the SIGTERM-143
# land bleed). This re-verification exists because a pid captured in the snapshot can EXIT before
# the kill, and macOS then hands that pid to somebody else. But it re-verified `ucomm` only, and
# `orphan-bash`/`stuck-wrapper`/`dead-lead-watchdog` all carry the ERE `^bash$` — so for the three
# classes that dominate the candidate set, the check asks "is the process now occupying this pid a
# bash?" and every recycled pid running a shell script answers yes. It is not a re-identification;
# it is a tautology.
#
# MEASURED, and it was live: `scripts/ship-land.sh` reported `verdict=killed signal=SIGTERM` on
# every re-land retry of 6 branches for 5 days (543 refs/land/failed pins since 2026-08-10). Of 118
# killed lands inside the reaper-log window, 97 (82%) died 2-10 s BEFORE a `garbage: N TERMed` line
# — median 3 s, which is exactly this arm's TERM→KILL `sleep 3`. The 15 s post-sweep shadows cover
# 2.10% of that window, so chance predicts ~2 of 118: the observed 97 is a 39x enrichment. And the
# lands died at ages the classifier cannot reach — `total_s` is the OUTER process's own age
# (LAND_T0, inherited by the locked child), and 117 of 157 kills were under the 600 s orphan-bash
# floor, with the shortest at 7 s. A 7-second-old process cannot be classified by an arm that
# requires 600; the pid it was handed could. This box allocates ~40 pids/s and wraps the whole
# 99999-pid space in ~41 min, while a sweep forks a `/bin/ps -p` per candidate between snapshot and
# kill — so a recycled candidate pid is not a corner case here, it is the weather.
#
# So the signature travels WITH the candidate: the argv the classifier actually decided on, checked
# against the argv the pid carries at signal time. A pid that changed hands fails it and is
# REFUSED, loudly. Kept as a containment substring (not equality) because the same helper serves
# the file-driven watchdog branch, whose identity is a name inside a longer command line, and
# because `ps -o args=` truncation must not manufacture a refusal. `-` opts out for a candidate
# whose argv was never observed, which is the fail-open this arm promises everywhere else.
_gs_verify_kill() { # <pid> <ucomm-ere> <TERM|KILL> [<argv-signature>] — re-verify comm AND argv, then
                    # signal. rc 0 = signalled.
  local pid="$1" re="$2" sig="$3" argsig="${4:--}" c a
  if _gs_is_ancestor "$pid"; then
    log "garbage: REFUSED $sig $pid — this sweep is running inside it (ancestor)"
    return 1
  fi
  if [ -n "${CC_REAPER_GARBAGE_KILL:-}" ]; then "$CC_REAPER_GARBAGE_KILL" "$pid" "$re" "$sig" "$argsig"; return $?; fi
  c="$(/bin/ps -p "$pid" -o ucomm= 2>/dev/null | sed 's/^ *//;s/ *$//')"
  [ -n "$c" ] || return 1
  printf '%s\n' "$c" | /usr/bin/grep -qE "$re" || return 1
  if [ "$argsig" != "-" ]; then
    a="$(/bin/ps -p "$pid" -o args= 2>/dev/null)"
    case "$a" in
      *"$argsig"*) : ;;
      *) log "garbage: REFUSED $sig $pid — argv no longer matches what was classified (pid reused): wanted <$argsig>, found <${a:-«gone»}>"
         return 1 ;;
    esac
  fi
  kill "-$sig" "$pid" 2>/dev/null
}
garbage_sweep() { # <reap:0|1> — reap the residue of dead sessions; see block comment above
  [ "${CC_REAPER_GARBAGE:-1}" = 1 ] || { log "garbage: disabled (CC_REAPER_GARBAGE=0)"; return 0; }
  local reap="${1:-0}" snapA snapB list cls pid re age argsig desc
  local nA=0 nkill=0
  _GS_T_PID=(); _GS_T_RE=(); _GS_T_SIG=()
  _gs_ancestors_init
  if [ -n "${CC_REAPER_GARBAGE_PS_A:-}" ] && [ -n "${CC_REAPER_GARBAGE_PS_B:-}" ]; then
    snapA="$(cat "$CC_REAPER_GARBAGE_PS_A" 2>/dev/null)" || snapA=""
    snapB="$(cat "$CC_REAPER_GARBAGE_PS_B" 2>/dev/null)" || snapB=""
  else
    snapA="$(_rp_bounded "${CC_REAPER_GARBAGE_PS_TIMEOUT_S:-30}" /bin/ps -Axo pid=,ppid=,etime=,ucomm= 2>/dev/null)" || snapA=""
    snapB="$(_rp_bounded "${CC_REAPER_GARBAGE_PS_TIMEOUT_S:-30}" /bin/ps -Axo pid=,args= 2>/dev/null)" || snapB=""
  fi
  [ -n "$snapA" ] || { log "garbage: snapshot unavailable — arm skipped (fail-open)"; return 0; }
  # One awk, both snapshots: emits `class<TAB>pid<TAB>ucomm-ERE<TAB>age<TAB>desc` per candidate.
  list="$(printf '%s\n===CUT===\n%s\n' "$snapA" "$snapB" | awk '
    # asig(pid) — the argv signature the kill-time re-identification checks against (see
    # _gs_verify_kill). Tabs are the field separator, so any in argv are folded to spaces; an
    # unobserved argv yields "-", which opts that candidate out rather than refusing it.
    # 120, not 60: at 60 the live log rendered `/Users/chrisren/Development/claude-infrastructure/scrip`
    # — the directory prefix alone is 57 chars, so every script in this repo logged IDENTICALLY and
    # the one question the line exists to answer (WHICH script did we just TERM?) was unanswerable.
    # It cost a whole diagnostic pass. Still bounded well inside what `ps -o args=` returns to a
    # captured (non-tty) stdout, so the kill-time containment check cannot start failing on
    # truncation — and a longer signature only makes that re-identification MORE specific.
    function asig(p,  s) { s = (p in args) ? args[p] : ""; if (s=="") return "-"
      gsub(/\t/, " ", s); return substr(s, 1, 120) }
    function esec(e,  d,a,n,hms) { d=0; if (index(e,"-")>0) { split(e,a,"-"); d=a[1]; e=a[2] }
      n=split(e,hms,":");
      if(n==3) return d*86400+hms[1]*3600+hms[2]*60+hms[3];
      if(n==2) return d*86400+hms[1]*60+hms[2];
      return d*86400+hms[1] }
    /^===CUT===$/ { part=2; next }
    part!=2 { if ($1 !~ /^[0-9]+$/) next
      pid=$1; nm=$4; for(i=5;i<=NF;i++) nm=nm" "$i
      parent[pid]=$2; secs[pid]=esec($3); comm[pid]=nm; next }
    { if ($1 !~ /^[0-9]+$/) next
      a=$2; for(i=3;i<=NF;i++) a=a" "$i; args[$1]=a }
    END {
      # mark every ANCESTOR of a live claude binary: nothing above a live session is touchable
      for (p in comm) if (comm[p]=="claude.exe" || args[p] ~ /node_modules\/\.bin\/claude/) cl[p]=1
      for (p in cl) { q=parent[p]; while (q!="" && q>1 && !(q in above)) { above[q]=1; q=parent[q] } }
      # THE LAND PATH IS NEVER GARBAGE (2026-08-16). `ship-land.sh` holds the landing lock and is
      # mid-push to trunk; `desk-land.sh` is its only sanctioned driver. Both are plain
      # launchd-parented bash once the desk backgrounds `handoff-fire.sh land …` (the non-blocking
      # path that the desk-land header itself offers), both run far past 600 s on a real gate, and
      # neither carried a whitelist token — so the arm classified an IN-PROGRESS LAND as the residue
      # of a dead session. That is the opposite of what this arm is for: the residue it collects is
      # by definition finished, and a land is the one orphan on this box still doing the work.
      # Verified as a fixture pair below (tests/cc-reaper.bats): the land shapes survive while an
      # unrelated orphan bash in the same closed world still dies, so the exemption cannot silently
      # widen into "nothing is collected".
      wl="lead-supervisor|cc-dispatch|cc-discover|cc-reaper|qos-census|compressor-sentinel|postland-verify|session-search|devserver-gc|deploy-live|cc-relogin|lr-reset-poller|teammate-reap|capacity-alarm|power-policy|log-rotation|restic|worktree-gc|caffeinate|cc-await-ping|mailbox-wake-arm|ship-land|desk-land|kitty|tmux|iTerm|launchd"
      for (p in comm) {
        if (p in above) continue
        if (comm[p]=="claude.exe") continue
        if (parent[p]==1) {
          if (comm[p]=="ps" && secs[p]>=60)                    { printf "orphan-ps\t%s\t^ps$\t%s\t%s\t-\n", p, secs[p], asig(p); continue }
          # THE WHITELIST BINDS HERE TOO (2026-08-17). It was consulted by `orphan-bash` alone, and
          # that is the wrong shape: `timeout`/`gtimeout` are on this list, and wrapping a bounded
          # command is exactly how a whitelisted daemon is normally invoked. So
          # `timeout -k 10 900 bash .../postland-verify.sh`, reparented to launchd, was classified
          # orphan-tool and TERMed at the 120 s floor — while the identical script invoked WITHOUT a
          # bound was protected at 600 s. The protection depended on whether someone had put a
          # timeout in front of it, which is not a property anyone reasoned about when adding a name
          # to `wl`. Measured on the live log after the land-path fix: no ship-land/desk-land is
          # classified any more, but `/opt/homebrew/bin/timeout -k 10 900 bash /Users/…/.cl…` was
          # still being reaped at age 488 s, and SIGTERM-143 land deaths continued at 8.4%.
          # `wl` is matched against the whole argv, so it reaches the wrapped command as intended.
          if (comm[p] ~ /^(awk|grep|egrep|tee|sed|cat|tail|wc|jq|sleep|gtimeout|timeout)$/ && args[p] !~ ("(" wl ")") && secs[p]>=120) { printf "orphan-tool\t%s\t^%s$\t%s\t%s\t-\n", p, comm[p], secs[p], asig(p); continue }
          if (comm[p]=="zsh" && args[p] ~ /^(-zsh|zsh|\/bin\/zsh)( |$)/ && secs[p]>=600) { printf "orphan-zsh\t%s\t^zsh$\t%s\t%s\t-\n", p, secs[p], asig(p); continue }
          if (comm[p]=="bash" && args[p] !~ ("(" wl ")") && args[p] !~ /lead-crash-watchdog/ && secs[p]>=600) { printf "orphan-bash\t%s\t^bash$\t%s\t%s\t%s\n", p, secs[p], asig(p), substr(args[p],1,80); continue }
        }
        if (comm[p]=="bash" && args[p] ~ /cc-close-attrib/ && secs[p]>=1800) printf "stuck-wrapper\t%s\t^bash$\t%s\t%s\t-\n", p, secs[p], asig(p)
      }
    }')"
  # lead-crash-watchdog daemons whose lead is DEAD — file-driven (the .pid file holds the lead pid,
  # the .daemon file its own), so a live session's watchdog can never match.
  local wdir="${CC_REAPER_WATCHDOG_DIR:-$HOME/.claude/watchdog}" df sid dpid lpid dargs
  for df in "$wdir"/*.daemon; do
    [ -f "$df" ] || continue
    sid="$(basename "$df" .daemon)"
    dpid="$(cat "$df" 2>/dev/null)"; lpid="$(cat "$wdir/$sid.pid" 2>/dev/null)"
    case "$dpid" in ''|*[!0-9]*) continue ;; esac
    case "$lpid" in ''|*[!0-9]*) continue ;; esac
    if ! kill -0 "$lpid" 2>/dev/null && kill -0 "$dpid" 2>/dev/null; then
      # The argv signature for this class is the daemon's NAME, not a snapshot prefix: the pid comes
      # from a file, so it is the candidate most exposed to a stale pid, and the name is exactly what
      # the `case` below already accepts as proof of identity.
      if [ -n "${CC_REAPER_GARBAGE_KILL:-}" ]; then
        list="$(printf '%s\n%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\t%s' "$list" "dead-lead-watchdog" "$dpid" "^bash$" "-" "lead-crash-watchdog" "sid:$sid")"
      else
        dargs="$(/bin/ps -p "$dpid" -o args= 2>/dev/null)"
        case "$dargs" in *lead-crash-watchdog*) list="$(printf '%s\n%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\t%s' "$list" "dead-lead-watchdog" "$dpid" "^bash$" "-" "lead-crash-watchdog" "sid:$sid")" ;; esac
      fi
    fi
  done
  [ -n "$list" ] || { log "garbage: 0 candidates"; return 0; }
  while IFS="$(printf '\t')" read -r cls pid re age argsig desc; do
    [ -n "$pid" ] || continue
    case "$pid" in *[!0-9]*) continue ;; esac
    nA=$((nA+1))
    if [ "$reap" = 1 ]; then
      # PER-CANDIDATE, ON THE PATH THAT ACTS. The reap branch used to log only the aggregate
      # (`garbage: N candidates, M TERMed`), so `grep orphan-bash` over 4.4 MB of live reaper log
      # returned ZERO lines while this arm was killing every land on the box — the detail existed
      # only on the dry-run branch, which is the one that changes nothing. An arm that signals
      # in anger must name what it signalled, or its log cannot answer "who killed this".
      log "garbage: TERM $cls pid=$pid age=${age}s argv=<$argsig>"
      _gs_verify_kill "$pid" "$re" TERM "$argsig" && {
        nkill=$((nkill+1))
        _GS_T_PID[${#_GS_T_PID[@]}]="$pid"; _GS_T_RE[${#_GS_T_RE[@]}]="$re"; _GS_T_SIG[${#_GS_T_SIG[@]}]="$argsig"
      }
    elif _gs_is_ancestor "$pid"; then
      # DRY-RUN must report what REAP would do. Without this the two modes disagree: the actuator
      # refuses an ancestor, and a dry run would still print it as a candidate to be reaped.
      say "  garbage (REFUSED — this sweep runs inside it): $cls pid=$pid age=${age}s $desc"
    else
      say "  garbage (would reap): $cls pid=$pid age=${age}s $desc"
    fi
  done <<EOF_GS
$list
EOF_GS
  # Parallel indexed arrays, not a space-joined `pid:re` string: the escalation now needs the argv
  # signature too, and an argv contains spaces — the very thing that string split on.
  if [ "$reap" = 1 ] && [ "${#_GS_T_PID[@]}" -gt 0 ]; then
    [ -n "${CC_REAPER_GARBAGE_KILL:-}" ] || sleep 3
    local i=0
    while [ "$i" -lt "${#_GS_T_PID[@]}" ]; do
      kill -0 "${_GS_T_PID[$i]}" 2>/dev/null \
        && _gs_verify_kill "${_GS_T_PID[$i]}" "${_GS_T_RE[$i]}" KILL "${_GS_T_SIG[$i]}"
      i=$((i+1))
    done
  fi
  log "garbage: $nA candidates, $nkill TERMed ($([ "$reap" = 1 ] && echo REAP || echo DRY-RUN))"
  say "garbage arm: $nA candidates, $nkill reaped"
  return 0
}

utc(){ date -u '+%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ' 2>/dev/null; }
# desk-board IDL rows — supervisor-shaped {ts,actor,kind,…}. Values pass as jq --arg/--argjson (NEVER
# string-interpolated into the filter) so a name/pane carrying quotes can't corrupt or inject the row.
# The abstain-monitor filters for .hook+.disposition, so these actor-tagged rows are safely ignored there.
idl_surface(){ # <cause> <name> <pane>
  mkdir -p "$(dirname "$IDL_FILE")" 2>/dev/null || true
  jq -nc --arg ts "$(utc)" --arg cause "$1" --arg name "$2" --arg pane "$3" \
    '{ts:$ts, actor:"cc-reaper", kind:"surface-page", cause:$cause, name:$name, pane:$pane}' \
    2>/dev/null >> "$IDL_FILE" || true
}
idl_selfcheck(){ # <live> <enumerated> <delta>
  mkdir -p "$(dirname "$IDL_FILE")" 2>/dev/null || true
  jq -nc --arg ts "$(utc)" --argjson live "$1" --argjson enum "$2" --argjson delta "$3" \
    '{ts:$ts, actor:"cc-reaper", kind:"selfcheck-page", live:$live, enumerated:$enum, delta:$delta}' \
    2>/dev/null >> "$IDL_FILE" || true
}
idl_stranded(){ # <pane> <name> <cause> <cwd> <branch> <ahead> <dirty> <firedBy> — the blockers-board row
  mkdir -p "$(dirname "$IDL_FILE")" 2>/dev/null || true
  jq -nc --arg ts "$(utc)" --arg pane "$1" --arg name "$2" --arg cause "$3" --arg cwd "$4" \
    --arg branch "$5" --argjson ahead "${6:-0}" --arg dirty "$7" --arg firedBy "$8" \
    '{ts:$ts, actor:"cc-reaper", kind:"stranded-work", pane:$pane, name:$name, cause:$cause,
      cwd:$cwd, branch:$branch, unlanded_commits:$ahead, tracked_dirty:$dirty, firedBy:$firedBy}' \
    2>/dev/null >> "$IDL_FILE" || true
}
idl_safeguard(){ # <pane> <name> <account> <model> <refusal> <firedBy> <recover_cmd> — the blockers-board row
  mkdir -p "$(dirname "$IDL_FILE")" 2>/dev/null || true
  jq -nc --arg ts "$(utc)" --arg pane "$1" --arg name "$2" --arg account "$3" \
    --arg model "$4" --arg refusal "$5" --arg firedBy "$6" --arg cmd "$7" \
    '{ts:$ts, actor:"cc-reaper", kind:"safeguard-blocked", pane:$pane, name:$name, account:$account,
      blocked_model:$model, refusal:$refusal, firedBy:$firedBy, recover_cmd:$cmd}' \
    2>/dev/null >> "$IDL_FILE" || true
}
# <message> [state-fingerprint] — best-effort desk page. ROLE-ADDRESSED by default (D2): cc-notify
# resolves the role file itself at send time AND follows a `.forward` chain / reroutes a dead target,
# none of which a locally-cat'd uuid can do. CC_PAGE_TO still forces one explicit pane.
#
# The role name + dir are DERIVED from PAGE_TO_FILE (dirname → CC_ROLES_DIR, basename → role) rather
# than hardcoded, so every existing seam keeps working unchanged: the default resolves to
# cc-roles/desk, tests/cc-reaper.bats' custom path resolves to its own dir, and /dev/null still
# disables (not a regular file ⇒ cc-notify exit 3 ⇒ no page).
#
# When a fingerprint is supplied, D7 damping suppresses an identical (target,fingerprint) re-send
# inside the TTL — a state CHANGE always gets through. A missing cc-notify is silent, never fatal.
#
# COMMS TRUTHFULNESS: the cc-notify RC is the outcome, and `|| true` is not a delivery. This used to
# discard it, so a page cc-notify REFUSED (rc 3 unresolvable target / rc 5 unwritable inbox) vanished
# with no log line, no IDL row and no retry — while the D7 marker written just before the attempt
# suppressed the next 30 min of re-sends. A refused page is now LOGGED and the marker dropped, so the
# next sweep re-sends. Exit: 0 = enqueued (or damped) · 1 = no cc-notify · 2 = the transport refused it.
#
# ── THERE IS NO DESK, AND THAT IS A SUPPORTED CONFIGURATION (2026-08-01) ─────────────────────────
# rc alone is STILL not the outcome. This machine runs no desk orchestrator: cc-roles/desk holds a
# 2026-07-26 iTerm2 pane uuid whose pane self-closed, the only organ that could create a successor is
# not loaded, and `cc-notify --role desk` therefore returns rc 0 with verdict=mailbox-only (or
# =unverified) FOREVER. Checking only `$?` made every such page a claimed delivery: handle_surface
# said "→ desk (surfaced, never-reap)" and wrote the per-(session,cause) damper, so a finding nothing
# alive had seen was ALSO silenced permanently. The three-way accounting documented above (marker
# withheld on a refusal) never engaged, because a dead desk never produces a refusal.
#
# So the outcome is partitioned exactly as scripts/lead-supervisor.sh (e5894631) partitions it:
#   REACHED   rc 0 + verdict=delivered  — a live session holds it. Claim the desk; keep the marker.
#   RECORDED  rc 0 + any other verdict  — enqueued to a mailbox with no proven reader. THE STEADY
#             STATE of a desk-less fleet, not a fault. The mailbox record stands (a desk registered
#             later drains it) and the marker is KEPT — re-paging every 300 s sweep is the 2026-07-19
#             composer storm this whole class exists to prevent — but nothing may SAY it reached a
#             desk, because nothing did.
#   REFUSED   rc != 0 (3 unresolvable · 5 inbox unwritable) — the transport took nothing at all.
#             Marker withheld ⇒ the next sweep genuinely retries. Unchanged from today.
# cc-notify emits its `verdict=` token on STDERR, so it is captured with 2>&1 and PARSED (memory:
# claimed-outcome-vs-checked-outcome — the token existed; nobody read it). A verdict we cannot read
# is a THIRD state (`unreadable`), never silently promoted to success.
notify_desk(){ # <message> [fingerprint]
  local msg="$1" fp="${2:-}" rdir rname rc=0 out=""
  NOTIFY_OUTCOME=no-channel; NOTIFY_VERDICT=""
  [ -n "$NOTIFY_BIN" ] || return 1
  if [ -n "$fp" ] && command -v damp_should_send >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    damp_should_send "${PAGE_TO:-role:$PAGE_TO_FILE}" "$fp" || { log "page-damped [$fp]"; NOTIFY_OUTCOME=damped; return 0; }
  fi
  if [ -n "$PAGE_TO" ]; then
    out="$("$NOTIFY_BIN" "$PAGE_TO" "$msg" 2>&1)"; rc=$?
  else
    rdir="$(dirname "$PAGE_TO_FILE")"; rname="$(basename "$PAGE_TO_FILE")"
    out="$(CC_ROLES_DIR="$rdir" "$NOTIFY_BIN" --role "$rname" "$msg" 2>&1)"; rc=$?
  fi
  NOTIFY_VERDICT="$(printf '%s' "$out" | grep -oE 'verdict=[A-Za-z-]+' | head -1 | cut -d= -f2)"
  [ -n "$NOTIFY_VERDICT" ] || NOTIFY_VERDICT=unreadable
  # ── REACHED ──
  if [ "$rc" = 0 ] && [ "$NOTIFY_VERDICT" = delivered ]; then NOTIFY_OUTCOME=reached; return 0; fi
  # ── RECORDED — enqueued, but no live reader. The desk-less steady state. ──
  if [ "$rc" = 0 ]; then
    NOTIFY_OUTCOME=recorded
    log "page RECORDED verdict=$NOTIFY_VERDICT target=${PAGE_TO:-role:$(basename "$PAGE_TO_FILE")} [$fp] — enqueued to the mailbox; NO live desk received it (no re-send: the record stands and re-paging every sweep is the storm)"
    return 0
  fi
  # ── REFUSED — the transport took nothing; retry next sweep. ──
  NOTIFY_OUTCOME=refused
  [ -n "$fp" ] && command -v damp_forget >/dev/null 2>&1 && damp_forget "${PAGE_TO:-role:$PAGE_TO_FILE}" "$fp"
  log "page SEND FAILED rc=$rc target=${PAGE_TO:-role:$(basename "$PAGE_TO_FILE")} — NOT delivered (cc-notify refused it); damping marker dropped so the next sweep retries"
  return 2
}
sanitize_key(){ printf '%s' "$1" | tr -c 'A-Za-z0-9._-' '_'; }   # marker-filename-safe damping key

# ── T-P3-4: was this pane FIRED as a self-retiring peer worker (vs started by the operator)? ──
# The ONLY writer is the spawner (handoff-fire.sh, at fire time — the one process that can know a
# session was fired rather than launched by a human). FAIL-SAFE BY CONSTRUCTION: no marker ⇒ treated
# as an operator/role session ⇒ never auto-reaped. Nothing here INFERS the marker from session
# state, so it cannot be forged by a session's own behaviour — an idle, clean, landed operator pane
# looks identical to a worker and is still never promoted. An operator's shell launch, `claude -w`,
# a --recycle continuation and a --no-self-retire fire all leave no marker.
# TENANCY (2026-07-24 rule 2): the stamp is pane-keyed and never expired with tenancy, so a later
# session reusing a previously-fired pane (a worker closed, the operator reopened it) inherited the
# stale "self-retiring worker" contract and could be auto-reaped. The stamp gates the CURRENT session
# only if that session BOOTED within CC_FIRED_BOOT_MAX_S of the fire; a startedAt beyond firedAt+boot-max
# is a different tenant → stale stamp (GC'd in the sweep). Unknown startedAt ⇒ INVALID (surface, not reap).
iso_to_epoch_r(){ # <iso8601> → epoch seconds (empty on parse fail). Mirrors cc-classify iso_to_epoch.
  local s="${1%%.*}"; s="${s%Z}"
  [ -z "$s" ] && return 0
  TZ=UTC date -j -f "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S" "$s" +%s 2>/dev/null || true
}
fired_at_epoch_r(){ # <paneUUID> → fire epoch (marker firedAt; fallback: marker file mtime)
  local f="$FIRED_DIR/${1:-}.json" iso ep
  [ -f "$f" ] || return 1
  iso="$(jq -r '.firedAt // ""' "$f" 2>/dev/null)"
  ep="$(iso_to_epoch_r "$iso")"
  [ -n "$ep" ] || ep="$(stat -f %m "$f" 2>/dev/null)"
  [ -n "$ep" ] || return 1
  printf '%s' "$ep"
}
# fired_stamp_state <paneUUID> <startedAt-ms> → prints none|invalid|stale|valid (always exit 0):
#   none    no UUID / no marker file · invalid marker present but startedAt unknown or firedAt
#   unparseable (fail to surface) · stale tenant booted > firedAt+BOOT_MAX after the fire (GC it) ·
#   valid   tenant booted within firedAt+BOOT_MAX (the fired worker itself).
fired_stamp_state(){
  local pane="${1:-}" started_ms="${2:-}" fep started_s
  [ -n "$pane" ] || { printf none; return 0; }
  # ADDRESS SHAPE: a safe filename component, NOT "hex-shaped". `hdl-<hex>` is a real pane
  # address (bin/cc-pane-headless:124/:197); the hex spelling refused it. SSOT rationale:
  # hooks/session-register.sh. Backlog 4b9d5e93b40a (writer) + 5d1b5dd9b3db (this consumer).
  case "$pane" in ''|.|..|.*|*[!A-Za-z0-9._-]*) printf none; return 0 ;; esac
  [ -f "$FIRED_DIR/$pane.json" ] || { printf none; return 0; }
  case "$started_ms" in ''|*[!0-9]*) printf invalid; return 0 ;; esac  # unknown startedAt ⇒ INVALID
  [ "$started_ms" -gt 0 ] || { printf invalid; return 0; }
  fep="$(fired_at_epoch_r "$pane" || true)"
  case "$fep" in ''|*[!0-9]*) printf invalid; return 0 ;; esac         # unparseable firedAt ⇒ INVALID
  started_s=$(( started_ms / 1000 ))
  if [ "$started_s" -le $(( fep + FIRED_BOOT_MAX_S )) ]; then printf valid; else printf stale; fi
  return 0
}
fired_peer(){ # <paneUUID> [startedAt-ms] → 0 iff a TENANCY-VALID spawner stamp gates this session
  [ "$(fired_stamp_state "${1:-}" "${2:-}")" = valid ]
}
fired_by(){ # <paneUUID> → the ORIGINATOR uuid that fired this pane (marker firedBy; empty if unmarked)
  local pane="${1:-}"
  [ -n "$pane" ] || return 0
  case "$pane" in ''|.|..|.*|*[!A-Za-z0-9._-]*) return 0 ;; esac
  jq -r '.firedBy // ""' "$FIRED_DIR/$pane.json" 2>/dev/null || true
}
# Gap-2 helpers for the coordination-abandoned / handed-off-lead second legs.
alive(){ [ -n "${1:-}" ] && kill -0 "$1" 2>/dev/null; }
find_transcript(){ # <sid> → its .jsonl path (first match across the account roots); mirrors cc-classify
  local sid="${1:-}" r f
  [ -n "$sid" ] || return 1
  for r in $PROJECT_ROOTS; do
    [ -d "$r" ] || continue
    f="$(find "$r" -maxdepth 2 -name "$sid.jsonl" 2>/dev/null | head -1)"
    [ -n "$f" ] && { printf '%s' "$f"; return 0; }
  done
  return 1
}
# Gap-3: the reaper's wall-clock. A file seam (CC_REAPER_NOW_FILE) lets a test advance it across the
# sweep deterministically (durable product, not an mtime); in production it is just `date +%s`.
reaper_now(){ if [ -n "${CC_REAPER_NOW_FILE:-}" ] && [ -r "$CC_REAPER_NOW_FILE" ]; then cat "$CC_REAPER_NOW_FILE" 2>/dev/null; else date +%s; fi; }
# mtime in epoch seconds — BSD first (this is a macOS-only janitor), GNU as the fallback, and a
# non-zero return rather than a 0 on failure: a caller comparing timestamps must be able to tell
# "cannot read" from "the epoch", or an unreadable file reads as infinitely stale. Mirrors the
# stat idiom already used for the lockdir age.
file_mtime_r(){ # <path> → epoch seconds | empty+1
  local p="${1:-}" m
  [ -n "$p" ] || return 1
  m="$(stat -f %m "$p" 2>/dev/null || stat -c %Y "$p" 2>/dev/null)" || return 1
  case "$m" in ''|*[!0-9]*) return 1 ;; esac
  printf '%s' "$m"
}
clear_fired_marker(){ # <paneUUID> — the pane is gone; drop its marker so the dir cannot grow forever
  local pane="${1:-}"
  [ -n "$pane" ] || return 0
  case "$pane" in ''|.|..|.*|*[!A-Za-z0-9._-]*) return 0 ;; esac
  rm -f "$FIRED_DIR/$pane.json" 2>/dev/null || true
}

# live_pane_count — INDEPENDENT count of live INTERACTIVE claude panes (the P0-12b truth signal). Mirrors
# claude-accounts.concurrency(): argv0 is the claude binary (or its cli.js), excluding headless one-shots
# (-p/--print/--version) and env-inherited node/MCP children. Deliberately NOT read from the cc-registry —
# the whole point is to catch panes the registry (hence cc-classify) never enumerated.
# argv0 forms accepted: `claude`, `*/claude`, `claude.exe`, `*/claude.exe`, `*cli.js`. The `.exe` binary is
# the eval-track (2.1.x claude-next) install's own name (…/@anthropic-ai/claude-code/bin/claude.exe) — a
# FIRST-CLASS interactive session, registered by session-register.sh:63 (claude|claude.exe|claude-*).
# Omitting it made this signal undercount live panes, biasing the live−enum delta in BOTH directions (a
# false-negative that desensitizes the blind-spot detector here, a false-positive Δ at other session mixes)
# — so this MUST stay in lockstep with the register/reconcile process-identity definition (cc-reconcile
# live_claude_pids carries the identical clause).
live_pane_count(){
  "$PS_BIN" -wwEo command= 2>/dev/null | awk '
    { t0=$1
      if (t0!="claude" && t0!="claude.exe" && t0 !~ /\/claude$/ && t0 !~ /\/claude\.exe$/ && t0 !~ /cli\.js/) next
      # LOCKSTEP with cc-reconcile live_claude_pids (spec 03 E12 — the two share ONE process-identity
      # definition, so the -p rule is changed in both or in neither). A RESIDENT headless agent carries
      # -p too; --input-format is what separates it from a one-shot `claude -p "hi"`, which must stay
      # uncounted. The marker scan spans argv rather than the narrow window because --input-format
      # lands at field 8 here in the real invocation. It stops at the first KEY=value field: this ps
      # runs with -E, so the ENVIRONMENT follows argv and a marker matched there would count one-shot
      # probes as panes. Breaking early only loses the marker — it fails toward the count of today.
      hasif=0
      for (i=2; i<=NF; i++) { if ($i ~ /^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*=/) break; if ($i=="--input-format") hasif=1 }
      for (i=2; i<=7 && i<=NF; i++) {
        if ($i=="--version") next
        if (($i=="-p" || $i=="--print") && !hasif) next
      }
      c++ }
    END { print c+0 }'
}

# clean AND the branch's content is on trunk — the load-bearing work-landed re-check (post-classify race
# guard). P0-17 landed-by-CONTENT (a18 L-10): a squash/cherry-pick land (different sha, same content)
# leaves HEAD "N ahead" by COUNT though the work is durably on trunk; a count check DEFERred it forever.
work_landed() {
  local cwd="$1"
  [ -d "$cwd" ] || return 1
  "$GIT_BIN" -C "$cwd" rev-parse --git-dir >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 1
  # --untracked-files=no (2026-07-20): a finished session's own work-safety is about ITS OWN
  # uncommitted TRACKED changes — never a sibling's untracked litter in a SHARED cwd. Counting
  # untracked files left every co-cwd session permanently "dirty" (one stray scratch file in the
  # shared checkout ⇒ landed never re-verified ⇒ nothing could self-close), which is how 13+
  # finished workers piled up on the operator's confirm-close queue. Untracked files are also
  # never LOST by a pane close (they stay on disk; worktree_cleanup separately refuses to
  # --force-remove a tree carrying any).
  [ -z "$("$GIT_BIN" -C "$cwd" status --porcelain --untracked-files=no 2>/dev/null)" ] || return 1
  local ahead; ahead="$("$GIT_BIN" -C "$cwd" rev-list --count "$TRUNK"..HEAD 2>/dev/null)" || return 1
  [ "${ahead:-1}" = 0 ] && return 0                              # fast path: 0 ahead by COUNT → landed
  # content path: `git cherry` patch-id equivalence ('+' = genuinely unlanded); tree-diff-0 = squash backstop.
  local cherry_out
  if cherry_out="$("$GIT_BIN" -C "$cwd" cherry "$TRUNK" HEAD 2>/dev/null)"; then
    printf '%s\n' "$cherry_out" | grep -q '^+' || return 0
  fi
  "$GIT_BIN" -C "$cwd" diff --quiet "$TRUNK" HEAD 2>/dev/null && return 0
  return 1
}

# ── WHAT `ahead=0` CONFLATES, AND THE TWO ORACLES THAT SEPARATE IT (backlog 7c22e9b43956) ────────
# work_landed's fast path answers "is anything unlanded?" with a COUNT, and `ahead=0` is true of two
# OPPOSITE states: "committed everything and landed it" and "NEVER COMMITTED ANYTHING". For the
# second, ahead=0 is not evidence of doneness — it IS the silence, which makes the reap's own audit
# string at the ACT step ("positive done-evidence, not inferred from silence") false exactly when it
# is relied upon. Measured on a real death: pane B2D1CE68, 2026-07-30T04:39Z, REAPed mid-Phase-1
# while blocked on its own background task, its whole work product only in its transcript — while
# THREE sibling finished-teammate sessions in the SAME sweep were DEFERred by the `[ "$landed" !=
# yes ]` guard, each because it HELD COMMITS. The protection was inverted with respect to the loss:
# weakest where the loss is total, strongest where the loss is smallest and recoverable.
# Forensics: docs/research/reaped-uncommitted-peer-2026-08-19.md.
#
# EVERY UNCERTAIN ANSWER IS 2 ("cannot tell") AND EVERY CALLER TREATS 2 AS "PROCEED AS BEFORE". That
# is the whole failure-direction design: a missed detection costs one extra operator page, an
# over-eager one would cost a silent reap, and this belt exists because a silent reap already
# happened once.

# THE DISCRIMINATOR IS THE WORKTREE'S OWN HEAD REFLOG, never the ahead-count. Measured on this box:
# a worktree holding 173 commits reads ahead=0 once landed AND carries 173 reflog `commit` entries;
# a freshly created worktree reads ahead=0 and carries none. Same ahead, opposite reflog.
# KNOWN AND DELIBERATE LIMITS, both failing toward the pre-existing behaviour (reap):
#   · a SHARED checkout's reflog is every session's, so it reads "committed" for everyone;
#   · a POOLED worktree re-used across sessions inherits its predecessor's entries.
# Neither can be fixed from a per-cwd oracle, and neither makes the belt worse than the status quo.
# Under-matching the commit-producing verbs below yields 1 ("never committed") ⇒ an extra page;
# over-matching yields 0 ⇒ today's silent reap. So the list is deliberately short, not exhaustive.
session_ever_committed() { # <cwd> → 0 committed HERE · 1 NEVER committed here · 2 cannot tell
  local cwd="${1:-}" gd rl n
  [ -n "$cwd" ] && [ -d "$cwd" ] || return 2
  gd="$("$GIT_BIN" -C "$cwd" rev-parse --git-dir 2>/dev/null)" || return 2
  [ -n "$gd" ] || return 2
  case "$gd" in /*) rl="$gd/logs/HEAD" ;; *) rl="$cwd/$gd/logs/HEAD" ;; esac   # rev-parse may answer relatively
  [ -f "$rl" ] || return 2                       # reflog off/absent ⇒ cannot tell, NEVER "never committed"
  # `grep -c` PRINTS 0 and EXITS 1 on no-match, so `$(grep -c … || echo 0)` yields the two-line
  # string "0\n0" and every numeric test downstream errors out. No `||` arm — the count is the answer.
  n="$(grep -cE "$(printf '\t')(commit|cherry-pick|rebase|merge|am|revert)" "$rl" 2>/dev/null)"
  case "${n:-}" in ''|*[!0-9]*) return 2 ;; esac
  [ "$n" -gt 0 ] && return 0
  return 1
}

peer_notify_back() { # <pane> → the back-channel ARMED AT FIRE TIME, empty when none was
  local s="$FIRED_DIR/${1:-}.json"
  [ -n "${1:-}" ] && [ -s "$s" ] || return 0
  command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 0
  jq -r '.notifyBack // empty' "$s" 2>/dev/null || true
}

# Did this pane ever announce to the address it was fired with? A SECOND, INDEPENDENT implementation
# of the read in handoff-fire.sh's sc_announce_before_retire — deliberately, for the same reason the
# 2026-07-24 belt re-derives cc-classify's verdict: that one runs only on the SELF-CLOSE path, and
# the reaper's entire population is the sessions for which self-close never ran. Sharing the code
# would not have shared the reach.
#   MATCH FIELDS 2..NF, never field 1 (the timestamp), and compare WHOLE fields: the record carries
#   the resolved key AND the as-given spelling, because recording only the resolved one made the
#   store unanswerable for the one question it exists to answer (cc-notify `_record_send` header).
#   A record whose every line has NF<=2 predates that fix and cannot answer either way ⇒ 2.
peer_ping_verdict() { # <pane> <armed-address> → 0 announced · 1 definitely NOT announced · 2 cannot tell
  local pane="${1:-}" nb="${2:-}" f rc=0
  [ -n "$pane" ] && [ -n "$nb" ] || return 2
  # POLICE THE DENOMINATOR: with no store at all, EVERY pane would read "never announced" and the
  # belt would fire on the whole fleet. A missing store is a blind instrument, not a fleet of silent
  # peers (memory: lookup-miss-is-not-absence, positive-control-the-denominator).
  [ -d "$MAILBOX_DIR/.sent" ] || return 2
  f="$MAILBOX_DIR/.sent/$pane"
  # …but with the store PRESENT, no file under this pane's key IS the definite negative: cc-notify
  # appends one line per successful enqueue, so a pane that ever announced has a file here.
  [ -e "$f" ] || return 1
  { [ -s "$f" ] && [ -r "$f" ]; } || return 2
  awk -v want="$nb" '
    { for (i = 2; i <= NF; i++) if ($i == want) { hit = 1; exit } }
    NF <= 2 { legacy = 1 }
    END { exit(hit ? 0 : (legacy ? 3 : 1)) }
  ' "$f" || rc=$?
  case "$rc" in 0) return 0 ;; 1) return 1 ;; 3) return 2 ;; *) return 2 ;; esac
}

# ── THE THIRD CHANNEL: POSITIVE EVIDENCE OF IN-FLIGHT WORK (backlog 7c22e9b43956, leg 3) ─────────
# Leg 2 above is deliberately narrowed to peers that OWED an announcement (a --notify-back was armed
# at fire time). A peer fired WITHOUT one never owed anything, so its silence carries no information,
# and widening leg 2 over it would be the exact polarity error sc_announce_before_retire avoids —
# reading "said nothing" as "did nothing". Measured cost of getting that wrong: the reaper's log
# holds 618 REAP lines over 173 distinct panes, and a belt firing across all of them re-creates the
# confirm-close pile-up the 2026-07-20 work_landed relaxation exists to undo.
#
# So this leg reads no silence at all. It asks the opposite question — is there POSITIVE evidence
# that this session still has work in flight? — on a channel that exists whether or not a ping was
# ever promised: the session's own Agent-tool subagents. A subagent runs IN-PROCESS, so tearing the
# pane down SIGKILLs it mid-run with its deliverable unwritten and nothing observes the loss. That is
# the same loss scripts/handoff-fire.sh subagent_gate refuses a --recycle over; this is a SECOND,
# INDEPENDENT implementation of its predicate, for the same reason the 2026-07-24 belt re-derives
# cc-classify's verdict — subagent_gate runs only on the self-close/recycle path, and the reaper's
# entire population is the sessions for which self-close never ran. Sharing the code would not have
# shared the reach.
#
# THE BARE PREDICATE IS NOT USABLE ON ITS OWN, AND THE MEASUREMENT IS WHY. "last quoted stop_reason
# is not end_turn" reads IN-FLIGHT for 133 of the 717 agent transcripts on this box — but 119 of
# those 133 were last written MORE THAN SEVEN DAYS AGO. They are corpses: subagents killed by an
# interrupted turn, or by their session dying around them. Wired bare, this leg would be a permanent
# refusal generator, which is the alarm that says as little as one that never fires. Two bounds fix
# it, and each was chosen from data rather than taste:
#
#   (1) ORDERING, not age (memory: log ordering proves which branch ran). While a subagent is
#       genuinely in flight the session CANNOT write its tool_result, so the subagent's transcript is
#       the newest write in the session dir. Once the session writes past it, that subagent has
#       returned or been abandoned. Measured: this retires 112 of the 133, leaving 21.
#   (2) A SILENCE CAP retires the remaining 21 — every one of them a session that DIED holding a
#       subagent (ages 9 h to 20 d). The cap is measured, not guessed: over 584 subagent transcripts
#       that provably ran to completion, 59,266 inter-record gaps give p99 = 89 s and p99.9 = 306 s,
#       and only 17 gaps (0.029%) exceed 900 s. The default therefore sits ~3x above p99.9.
#
# FAILURE DIRECTION, STATED PLAINLY: the cap can only ever make this leg ABSTAIN. A genuinely live
# subagent that has been silent longer than the cap yields "cannot tell" and the sweep proceeds
# exactly as it does today — this leg can under-refuse, never over-refuse. That is deliberate: an
# over-eager refusal here is a permanent un-reapable pane, and the pile-up is the failure mode with
# no natural end.
#
# EXISTENCE EVIDENCE that the composite predicate can fire at all: for each of the 21 sessions that
# died holding an in-flight subagent, the gap between that subagent's last write and its session's
# last write was 0-183 s — inside the cap. Had a sweep run at that moment, this leg would have fired
# for every one of them.
#
# WHAT THIS LEG DOES NOT COVER, so nobody later mistakes it for the whole item: the B2D1CE68 victim
# was blocked on a backgrounded Bash TASK, not a subagent. That store
# (/private/tmp/claude-501/<slug>/<sid>/tasks/*.output) is reboot-ephemeral and records no
# started/finished status, so it cannot answer the question. The obvious session-level analogue —
# "the session's own last stop_reason is not end_turn" — was measured and REFUTED: 253 of 401
# sampled session transcripts read that way, so a belt on it would refuse ~63% of all reaps.
session_subagent_inflight() { # <sid> → 0 in-flight · 1 definitely none · 2 cannot tell
  local sid="${1:-}" tj sdir now sm am f stop
  [ -n "$sid" ] || return 2
  tj="$(find_transcript "$sid" 2>/dev/null)" || return 2
  [ -n "$tj" ] && [ -f "$tj" ] || return 2
  sdir="${tj%.jsonl}"
  # No subagents/ dir at all = this session never spawned one. A genuine negative, not an unresolved
  # one — the same ruling subagent_gate makes about its own common case.
  [ -d "$sdir/subagents" ] || return 1
  sm="$(file_mtime_r "$tj")" || return 2
  case "$sm" in ''|*[!0-9]*) return 2 ;; esac
  now="$(reaper_now)"
  case "$now" in ''|*[!0-9]*) return 2 ;; esac
  for f in "$sdir"/subagents/agent-*.jsonl; do
    [ -f "$f" ] || continue                       # unmatched glob, or a meta with no transcript
    # A FINISHED agent's last quoted stop_reason is "end_turn" — that IS the stop. `grep -o | tail -1`
    # takes the LAST one, which skips streaming `"stop_reason":null` partials and stays correct for a
    # RESUMED agent (its old end_turn is not the last). jq-free on purpose: this must survive
    # PATH=/usr/bin:/bin under launchd (memory: hermetic-in-stubs-not-in-interpreter).
    stop="$(grep -o '"stop_reason":"[a-z_]*"' "$f" 2>/dev/null | tail -1)" || true
    case "$stop" in *'"end_turn"') continue ;; esac
    am="$(file_mtime_r "$f")" || continue
    case "$am" in ''|*[!0-9]*) continue ;; esac
    [ "$am" -ge "$sm" ] 2>/dev/null || continue                       # bound (1): the session wrote past it
    [ "$(( now - am ))" -le "$INFLIGHT_MAX_SILENCE_S" ] 2>/dev/null || continue   # bound (2)
    return 0
  done
  return 1
}

# ── THE FOURTH CHANNEL: THE PEER'S OWN UNMET CONTRACT (backlog 7c22e9b43956, leg 4) ──────────────
# Legs 2 and 3 leave a hole the row's own falsifier sits in. Leg 2 can only speak where a
# --notify-back was ARMED (a peer fired without one never owed a ping, so its silence carries no
# information) and leg 3 only where the session spawned an Agent-tool subagent. A dispatched peer
# with NEITHER — 0 commits, no back-channel, no subagent, clean tree, idle past the settle — still
# falls straight through to REAP on ahead=0-by-silence. Measured 2026-08-19: 459 of 603 fired stamps
# on this box carry no notifyBack at all, so that hole is 76% of the fired population (re-measured
# 2026-08-20: 516 of 618 .json stamps, 83% — the hole is growing, not closing).
#
# 🚨 THAT 83% IS THE SIZE OF THE HOLE, NOT THIS LEG'S COVERAGE OF IT, and the two were conflated in
# this comment for one commit. Re-measured 2026-08-20 (recycle #58) over every no-notifyBack stamp
# whose transcript still exists (n=344): 341 carry NO goal record at all, 2 read live-and-unmet, 1
# read met/failed. Leg 4 can therefore speak for ~1% of the hole, not for the hole. Dating the
# population against the 2026-08-09 goal-in-the-recipe cutover does NOT rescue it — post-cutover the
# split is 66 no-goal / 2 live / 1 resolved (n=69), i.e. 95% still silent. Cross-checked against the
# real library rather than this file's own parse: goal_live_condition answers live for exactly 2 of
# those 69, the same 2. So "`--goal` is part of the fire recipe" is true of the FIRE and false of
# the RECORD — a goal_status attachment only exists once the evaluator has run, and a dispatched
# peer that self-retires quickly never produces one. This leg is correct and cheap and it closes the
# sub-case it names; it is NOT the remedy for the row's falsifier at large, and the residual — a
# fired peer with no commits, no back-channel, no subagent AND no goal record — is still unbelted.
#
# THE CHANNEL IS THE GOAL, AND IT IS POSITIVE EVIDENCE, NOT SILENCE. Since 2026-08-09 `--goal` is
# part of the fire recipe rather than an option, so a dispatched peer carries a machine-readable
# contract: an end state, re-judged by the evaluator after every turn, whose verdict is written into
# the peer's own transcript as a goal_status attachment. A goal that is neither met nor failed is the
# session stating, in its own record, that it is NOT done. Reaping over that destroys work whose only
# home is the transcript — which is exactly how pane B2D1CE68 died.
#
# THE INSTRUMENT WAS ALREADY BUILT AND SIMPLY NOT ON THIS PATH. hooks/lib/goal-state.sh's
# goal_live_condition is already consumed by session-continue, mailbox-drain, validate-bash,
# wrap-ledger, handoff-fire and cc-await-ping. The reaper — the one actor that can DESTROY the
# session it describes — was the only consumer missing. Sourced above with the same resolve order and
# the same fail-closed contract as context-econ.sh.
#
# WHY THE CORPSE FAILURE MODE DOES NOT REACH HERE, which is what made leg 3 need two bounds and this
# need none. Over all FIVE account transcript roots (3,386 transcripts — a two-root census reads 51%
# and its shortfall renders as a real absent population), 484 carry a goal record: 267 read
# LIVE-and-UNMET and 217 read met/failed, so the channel reads BOTH ways rather than always-on. 151
# of those 267 are more than seven days old — the corpse shape that would have made a bare predicate
# a permanent-refusal generator. They cannot reach this belt: a corpse is a session whose process is
# DEAD, and cc-classify labels those `crashed`, which is never auto-reaped. This belt only ever runs
# for finished / finished-teammate, both of which require a live pid. Verified on the live population
# rather than argued: of 9 live sessions, 2 read GOAL-LIVE-UNMET and 7 do not, and neither of the 2
# is in this belt's cause set.
#
# HONEST LIMIT: the belt's true population — finished/finished-teammate with 0 commits and no
# back-channel — was EMPTY at the time of writing, so this leg's false-positive rate on its own
# population is UNMEASURED, not measured-and-low. It ships behind its own kill-switch for that
# reason. The failure directions are not symmetric and that is what settles the default: a wrong
# REFUSAL costs one damped desk surface with the pane left alive, a wrong REAP destroys a work
# product that exists nowhere else.
#
# THREE-VALUED, and the two negatives are kept apart. goal_live_condition returns 1 for "no goal was
# ever armed", for "met/failed", AND for "jq is missing / the file is unreadable" — conflating an
# unresolved read with a genuine negative is the exact sin legs 2 and 3 avoid, so the hard
# dependencies are checked HERE before its rc is trusted as an answer.
session_goal_inflight() { # <sid> → 0 in-flight (a /goal is LIVE and UNMET) · 1 definitely none · 2 cannot tell
  local sid="${1:-}" tj
  [ -n "$sid" ] || return 2
  command -v goal_live_condition >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 2   # lib absent ⇒ cannot tell, never "none"
  command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 2                    # the probe's own hard dependency
  tj="$(find_transcript "$sid" 2>/dev/null)" || return 2
  [ -n "$tj" ] && [ -r "$tj" ] || return 2
  goal_live_condition "$tj" >/dev/null 2>&1 && return 0
  return 1
}

checkpoint_first() { # <cwd> <name> <sid> — snapshot WIP to refs/wip/<name>/LAST (proven plumbing)
  local cwd="$1" name="$2" sid="$3"
  [ -x "$CHECKPOINT_BIN" ] || { log "checkpoint: $CHECKPOINT_BIN not executable — SKIP (no snapshot)"; return 1; }
  printf '{"session_id":"%s","hook_event_name":"TeammateIdle","cwd":"%s","team_name":"cc-reaper","teammate_name":"%s"}' \
    "$sid" "$cwd" "$name" | "$CHECKPOINT_BIN" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
  return 0
}

worktree_cleanup() { # <cwd> — remove a LINKED worktree after a confirmed teardown (never the main tree)
  local cwd="$1"
  case "$cwd" in /tmp/wt-*|/private/tmp/wt-*|/tmp/worktree-*|*/.worktrees/*) ;; *) return 0 ;; esac
  # Only a LINKED worktree (git-dir != <cwd>/.git). Removing must run from the MAIN worktree.
  local gitdir main
  gitdir="$("$GIT_BIN" -C "$cwd" rev-parse --git-dir 2>/dev/null)" || return 0
  case "$gitdir" in */worktrees/*) ;; *) return 0 ;; esac      # not a linked worktree → leave it
  main="$("$GIT_BIN" -C "$cwd" worktree list --porcelain 2>/dev/null | awk '/^worktree /{print $2; exit}')"
  [ -n "$main" ] && [ "$main" != "$cwd" ] || return 0
  # UNTRACKED GUARD (2026-07-20, paired with the --untracked-files=no relaxation in work_landed):
  # `worktree remove --force` DELETES untracked files. work_landed now (correctly) ignores them, so
  # without this check a reap could reach here and destroy an uncommitted research report / scratch
  # output nothing ever committed. HERE — and only here, where deletion is real — untracked counts
  # as dirty: leave the tree on disk and say so. The PANE is still reaped either way.
  if [ -n "$("$GIT_BIN" -C "$cwd" status --porcelain 2>/dev/null)" ]; then
    say "    worktree LEFT INTACT (untracked files present — removal would delete them): $cwd"
    log "worktree left intact (untracked present): $cwd"; return 0
  fi
  # ── RUNAWAY BOUND (P3, 2026-08-15) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # The per-sweep cap is what makes an unattended destructive actuator survivable. Every gate
  # above is a judgment about ONE worktree, and the whole class of failure that put this file in
  # MASTER_FLEET_FOOTPRINT §P3 is a sweep in which that judgment is systematically wrong — a
  # trunk that moved, a classify verdict built on stale evidence, a `$TRUNK` that resolves
  # somewhere unexpected. Per-item correctness cannot bound a systematic error; only a count can.
  # Past the cap the reaper stops REMOVING and says so, loudly, once. Panes are still torn down:
  # a directory left on disk is recoverable by definition, and this is a brake, not a stop.
  WT_REMOVED_THIS_SWEEP=$((WT_REMOVED_THIS_SWEEP + 1))
  if [ "$WT_REMOVE_MAX" -ge 0 ] && [ "$WT_REMOVED_THIS_SWEEP" -gt "$WT_REMOVE_MAX" ]; then
    if [ "$WT_REMOVED_THIS_SWEEP" = "$((WT_REMOVE_MAX + 1))" ]; then
      say "    ⚠️ worktree removal BOUND HIT ($WT_REMOVE_MAX this sweep) — every further tree is LEFT INTACT."
      say "       A sweep that wants to remove more than $WT_REMOVE_MAX worktrees is making a systematic"
      say "       judgment, not $WT_REMOVE_MAX individual ones. Inspect before raising CC_REAPER_WT_REMOVE_MAX."
      log "worktree removal bound hit: max=$WT_REMOVE_MAX — refusing further removals this sweep"
    fi
    say "    worktree LEFT INTACT (removal bound $WT_REMOVE_MAX reached this sweep): $cwd"
    log "worktree left intact (bound $WT_REMOVE_MAX): $cwd"
    return 0
  fi
  # ── THE BLAST RADIUS, READ BEFORE THE REMOVAL ────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # `git status --porcelain` — the guard directly above — is BLIND to ignored content, and
  # `git worktree remove` deletes it anyway, at exit 0, with no --force and no warning (measured
  # 2026-07-30 in scripts/worktree-gc.sh:ignored_inventory — a worktree holding only `secrets.env`
  # removes clean and the file is gone). It is not refused on, for the reason recorded there: the
  # live population is dominated by `node_modules`/`__pycache__`/`.claude-plans`, so refusing
  # would make this arm inert by construction. It is NAMED instead — read here, before removal,
  # because afterwards the answer is unrecoverable, which is exactly why nothing has ever
  # accounted for it. This is the ONE trace that these bytes existed.
  local ignored head branch
  ignored="$("$GIT_BIN" -C "$cwd" status --porcelain --ignored 2>/dev/null \
             | sed -n 's/^!! //p' | sort | tr '\n' ',' | sed 's/,$//')"
  head="$("$GIT_BIN" -C "$cwd" rev-parse HEAD 2>/dev/null)"
  branch="$("$GIT_BIN" -C "$cwd" rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD 2>/dev/null)"
  # NO --force (P3, 2026-08-15). It shipped here and nowhere else: scripts/worktree-gc.sh removes
  # on three separate paths and never forces, its test suite guards the SOURCE against forcing
  # (tests/worktree-gc.bats "the no-force / no-D discipline is in the source"), and audit §8-H is
  # the ruling — git's refusal is a second gate on OUR evidence, and forcing discards it. The tree
  # is proven clean four lines up, so --force overrode nothing here except git's own opinion, on
  # the one actuator that runs unattended and therefore has nobody to ask.
  if "$GIT_BIN" -C "$main" worktree remove "$cwd" 2>/dev/null; then
    say "    worktree removed: $cwd"; log "worktree removed: $cwd"
    [ -n "$ignored" ] && say "      └ gitignored content destroyed with it (git records this nowhere else): $ignored"
    log_worktree_disposal "$cwd" "$branch" "$head" "$ignored"
  else
    say "    worktree remove REFUSED by git (left intact — it changed since the gates ran): $cwd"
    log "worktree remove refused: $cwd"
  fi
}

log_worktree_disposal() { # <path> <branch> <head> <ignored-csv>
  # Appends to the SAME ledger scripts/worktree-gc.sh writes, deliberately: the operator's question
  # months later is "what was in that directory", never "which of my two reapers removed it", and
  # two ledgers would answer it only if you already knew which to open. `actor` is what tells them
  # apart. Best-effort by construction — a removal that already happened must never be un-recorded
  # because a log directory was missing, so this can only ever fail silently, never block.
  local dir esc_path esc_branch esc_ign
  dir="$(dirname "$WT_DISPOSAL_LOG")"
  mkdir -p "$dir" 2>/dev/null || return 0
  esc_path="$(printf '%s' "${1:-}" | sed 's/\\/\\\\/g; s/"/\\"/g')"
  esc_branch="$(printf '%s' "${2:-}" | sed 's/\\/\\\\/g; s/"/\\"/g')"
  esc_ign="$(printf '%s' "${4:-}" | sed 's/\\/\\\\/g; s/"/\\"/g')"
  printf '{"ts":"%s","event":"worktree-disposed","actor":"cc-reaper","path":"%s","branch":"%s","head":"%s","owner_proof":"teardown-verified+work-landed","preserved_at":"refs/heads/%s","destroyed_ignored":"%s"}\n' \
    "$(date -u '+%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ' 2>/dev/null)" "$esc_path" "$esc_branch" "${3:-}" "$esc_branch" "$esc_ign" \
    >> "$WT_DISPOSAL_LOG" 2>/dev/null || true
}

# ── T-P3-3: page the desk on a never-reap-but-surface cause (damped). Prints the reaper's per-session line. ──
handle_surface() { # <reap> <key> <name> <cause> <pane> <detail>
  local reap="$1" key="$2" name="$3" cause="$4" pane="$5" detail="$6"
  if [ "$reap" = 0 ]; then
    say "  WOULD-PAGE $name [$cause] — surface-worthy (desk paged in --reap)"; return 0
  fi
  mkdir -p "$PAGEDIR" 2>/dev/null || true
  local mk="$PAGEDIR/$key.cause" last
  last="$(cat "$mk" 2>/dev/null || true)"
  if [ "$last" = "$cause" ]; then
    say "  keep   $name [$cause] — surfaced (already paged this cause; damped)"; return 0
  fi
  # fingerprint = the STATE words only (session + cause). ${detail} is deliberately excluded: it
  # carries volatile text (times, counts) that would change every sweep and silently defeat damping.
  local nrc=0
  notify_desk "⚠️ REAPER SURFACE — session ${name} is ${cause}: ${detail} (never-reap; desk/operator resolves — re-engage a hang · close a crashed pane · confirm-close a shared-review). cc-reaper never auto-acts on this." \
              "surface:${name}:${cause}" || nrc=$?
  local outcome="${NOTIFY_OUTCOME:-unknown}"
  # The FINDING is recorded either way — the classification is true whether or not anyone was reached.
  # The DELIVERY claim is not, and $mk is this sweep's "already paged this cause" damper. Three
  # outcomes, deliberately NOT collapsed:
  #   0 REFUSED-BY-TRANSPORT is the only one that withholds the marker — the send is retryable and
  #     damping it would suppress every retry of a page the desk never got (the F1 defect class).
  #   · NO-CHANNEL-WIRED keeps the marker (unchanged): a missing cc-notify is static config, not a
  #     transient fault, so retrying it every sweep only floods the IDL — but the line must stop
  #     saying "→ desk", because nothing went there.
  #   · RECORDED (rc 0, verdict ≠ delivered) keeps the marker for exactly the same reason — a fleet
  #     with no desk registered is static config too, and re-paging it every 300 s sweep is the
  #     composer storm — but it likewise must stop saying "→ desk": the page sits in a mailbox no
  #     live session has read.
  # Retry cost is bounded by the sweep cadence (launchd, 300s): a standing refusal costs ~12 attempts
  # + IDL rows an hour, which is the correct volume for "the desk role is unset and a session needs
  # attention" — the same S-4 posture as the supervisor's per-sweep page record.
  idl_surface "$cause" "$name" "$pane"
  if [ "$nrc" = 2 ]; then
    say "  PAGE!  $name [$cause] — surfaced, but the desk page was REFUSED (cc-notify rc=2); retrying next sweep"
    log "surface-page UNDELIVERED $name [$cause] pane=$pane — cc-notify refused it; marker withheld, will retry"
    return 0
  fi
  printf '%s\n' "$cause" > "$mk" 2>/dev/null || true
  case "$outcome" in
    reached)
      say "  PAGE   $name [$cause] → desk (surfaced, never-reap)"
      log "surface-page DELIVERED $name [$cause] pane=$pane" ;;
    recorded)
      say "  SURFACE $name [$cause] — recorded to the desk mailbox; NO live desk received it (verdict=$NOTIFY_VERDICT)"
      log "surface-page RECORDED $name [$cause] pane=$pane verdict=$NOTIFY_VERDICT — no desk is registered; the finding stands in the IDL + mailbox, nothing live has read it" ;;
    damped)
      say "  SURFACE $name [$cause] — recorded; page suppressed (identical page inside the damping TTL)"
      log "surface-page DAMPED $name [$cause] pane=$pane — identical (target,fingerprint) inside the TTL" ;;
    *)
      say "  SURFACE $name [$cause] — recorded, NOT paged (no desk channel wired)"
      log "surface-page NO-CHANNEL $name [$cause] pane=$pane — cc-notify unavailable; recorded to the IDL only" ;;
  esac
}

# ── safeguard-blocked (2026-07-25): surface a fired peer that model content-safeguards refused on
#    arrival — a dead-on-arrival block, never a "done" session. Four things, none of which the generic
#    desk-only handle_surface does: (1) notify the ORIGINATOR (firedBy — the pane that fired it, read
#    from the fired marker) so the session that spawned it learns the brief never ran; (2) page the
#    desk (damped, same cadence as other surface causes); (3) write a blockers-board row carrying the
#    recovery command; (4) ONLY if the operator opted in (CC_REAPER_SAFEGUARD_AUTORECOVER=1), invoke
#    the recovery helper. NEVER reaps — recovery is a re-fire on a different model + a sanctioned
#    self-close, not a teardown. Both pages are D7-damped (one per (target, pane) inside the TTL).
handle_safeguard_blocked(){ # <reap> <obj-json>
  local reap="$1" o="$2"
  local pane name account model refusal firedBy recover_cmd
  pane="$(printf '%s' "$o" | jq -r '.paneUUID // ""')"
  name="$(printf '%s' "$o" | jq -r '.name // .paneUUID // "?"')"
  account="$(printf '%s' "$o" | jq -r '.account // "?"')"
  model="$(printf '%s' "$o" | jq -r '.blocked_model // ""')"
  refusal="$(printf '%s' "$o" | jq -r '.refusal // ""')"
  firedBy="$(fired_by "$pane")"
  recover_cmd="cc-recover-safeguard $pane"
  if [ "$reap" = 0 ]; then
    say "  WOULD-SURFACE $name [safeguard-blocked] — notify originator ${firedBy:-<none>} + board row + desk page (in --reap); recover: $recover_cmd"
    return 0
  fi
  # 1. ORIGINATOR page (firedBy), damped by (originator, pane) — the session that fired this needs to know.
  if [ -n "$firedBy" ] && [ -n "$NOTIFY_BIN" ]; then
    if ! command -v damp_should_send >/dev/null 2>&1 || damp_should_send "$firedBy" "safeguard:$pane"; then
      "$NOTIFY_BIN" "$firedBy" "⚠️ SAFEGUARD-BLOCKED — your fired peer ${name} (${account}${model:+, ${model}}) was refused by model content-safeguards on arrival and is idle DOA — its brief never ran. Refusal: ${refusal}. Recover (re-fire on another model): ${recover_cmd}" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
      log "safeguard originator-paged $firedBy for $pane"
    else
      log "safeguard originator page damped [$firedBy:$pane]"
    fi
  else
    log "safeguard-blocked $pane has no firedBy originator — desk + board only"
  fi
  # 2. desk page (damped) — same cadence as other surface causes.
  notify_desk "⚠️ REAPER SURFACE — ${name} is safeguard-blocked (${account}${model:+, ${model}}): model content-safeguards refused its first turn — idle DOA, brief never ran. Originator ${firedBy:-<unknown>} notified. Recover: ${recover_cmd}. cc-reaper never auto-acts on this." "surface:${name}:safeguard-blocked" || true
  # Name the desk leg by what actually happened to it — the summary line below is the operator-facing
  # claim, and "+ desk" is false in the desk-less configuration (rc 0, verdict=mailbox-only).
  local dnote
  case "${NOTIFY_OUTCOME:-unknown}" in
    reached)  dnote="desk" ;;
    recorded) dnote="desk MAILBOX (no live desk; verdict=$NOTIFY_VERDICT)" ;;
    damped)   dnote="desk (page damped inside the TTL)" ;;
    refused)  dnote="desk NOT reached (transport refused)" ;;
    *)        dnote="no desk channel wired" ;;
  esac
  # 3. blockers-board row (carries the recovery command for the one-glance operator view).
  idl_safeguard "$pane" "$name" "$account" "$model" "$refusal" "$firedBy" "$recover_cmd"
  # 4. opt-in auto-recovery (default OFF — surface-and-let-the-desk-decide).
  if [ "$SAFEGUARD_AUTORECOVER" = 1 ] && [ -n "$RECOVER_BIN" ]; then
    say "  AUTO-RECOVER $name [safeguard-blocked] → $RECOVER_BIN $pane --execute (operator opted in)"
    log "safeguard auto-recover $pane via $RECOVER_BIN"
    "$RECOVER_BIN" "$pane" --execute >/dev/null 2>&1 || log "safeguard auto-recover FAILED for $pane"
  fi
  say "  SURFACE $name [safeguard-blocked] → originator ${firedBy:-<none>} + ${dnote} + board (autorecover=$SAFEGUARD_AUTORECOVER)"
  log "safeguard-blocked surfaced $name pane=$pane firedBy=${firedBy:-none} desk=${NOTIFY_OUTCOME:-unknown} autorecover=$SAFEGUARD_AUTORECOVER"
}

# ── STRANDED WORK (2026-08-19, backlog 1b19ab3096d2 leg 2) ──────────────────────────────────────
# The LEAD-SIDE half of the succession contract, for the case where no close path runs at all.
# handoff-fire's self-close preflight already routes the committed-vs-landed fact to the originator
# (4a455e43b) — but a fired session that CRASHED, was killed, or whose pane died never reaches
# self-close, and this reaper is then the last actor that can still see its branch.
#
# The verdict was already being computed and then thrown away. `landed != yes` on a REAPABLE cause is
# exactly "this session is done and its work is not on trunk", and its whole disposition was `say`
# (the stdout of a launchd job) + `log` (a file with no consumer) + `continue`. A measurement
# delivered to nobody is indistinguishable from one never taken. The incident is bs-footer-motion
# (2026-08-09): 2 commits + 1 dirty file stranded, the lead read the track as COMPLETE, and only an
# operator screenshot ever surfaced it.
#
# Deliberately NOT handle_surface, for two reasons that are the whole point of this row:
#   · that page names the wrong remedies — "re-engage a hang · close a crashed pane · confirm-close a
#     shared-review" — none of which is "land the branch", and
#   · it pages the DESK only. The party that must learn is the ORIGINATOR: the session that read this
#     track as finished is the one holding a false belief, and it is the one that can still act.
# handle_safeguard_blocked exists for exactly this reason and is the shape copied here.
#
# EVIDENCE IS RE-DERIVED AT ACT TIME, never taken from the classify verdict. Two independent reasons:
# the verdict is a snapshot that may be minutes stale (the same race the promotion leg's work_landed
# re-check guards), and it is a bare yes/no that carries neither the branch nor the counts a recipient
# needs to act. `landed != yes` is also reachable for causes that are NOT stranded work at all — a
# non-git cwd, a git invocation that failed. This page ACTS on a negative, so it must positively
# CONFIRM the stranded state before firing: no unlanded commits AND no tracked dirt ⇒ nothing to say.
handle_stranded(){ # <reap> <name> <cause> <pane> <cwd> <idle> → 0 always (best-effort; never reaps)
  local reap="$1" name="$2" cause="$3" pane="$4" cwd="$5" idle="$6"
  local br="" ahead=0 dirty=no firedBy inspect_cmd
  [ -n "$cwd" ] && [ -d "$cwd" ] || return 0
  "$GIT_BIN" -C "$cwd" rev-parse --git-dir >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 0
  ahead="$("$GIT_BIN" -C "$cwd" rev-list --count "$TRUNK"..HEAD 2>/dev/null || echo 0)"
  case "$ahead" in ''|*[!0-9]*) ahead=0 ;; esac
  # TRACKED dirt only — the same definition work_landed itself uses. Counting untracked litter here
  # would let a live sibling's scratch file in a shared checkout page the originator of an innocent
  # session, which is the exact false-conviction the --untracked-files=no relaxation removed.
  [ -z "$("$GIT_BIN" -C "$cwd" status --porcelain --untracked-files=no 2>/dev/null)" ] || dirty=yes
  # PRESENCE CONFIRMED, or say nothing at all.
  if [ "$ahead" -eq 0 ] && [ "$dirty" = no ]; then
    log "stranded-check $name pane=$pane: not-landed verdict but tree shows 0 unlanded commits + no tracked dirt — nothing to surface"
    return 0
  fi
  # A land is not a strand. Under STRANDED_S this session is most likely mid-`ship-land`.
  if ! [ "${idle:-0}" -ge "$STRANDED_S" ] 2>/dev/null; then
    say "  keep   $name [$cause] — unlanded (${ahead} commit(s), dirty=${dirty}) but idle ${idle}s < stranded ${STRANDED_S}s (a land takes minutes)"
    log "stranded-hold $name pane=$pane ahead=$ahead dirty=$dirty idle=$idle < $STRANDED_S"
    return 0
  fi
  br="$("$GIT_BIN" -C "$cwd" rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD 2>/dev/null || true)"
  firedBy="$(fired_by "$pane")"
  inspect_cmd="git -C $cwd log --oneline $TRUNK..HEAD"
  if [ "$reap" = 0 ]; then
    say "  WOULD-SURFACE $name [$cause] — STRANDED: ${ahead} unlanded commit(s) on ${br:-?}, tracked-dirty=${dirty}; would notify originator ${firedBy:-<none>} + desk + board (in --reap)"
    return 0
  fi
  # 1. ORIGINATOR page, damped by (originator, pane) — the session holding the false "complete" belief.
  if [ -n "$firedBy" ] && [ -n "$NOTIFY_BIN" ]; then
    if ! command -v damp_should_send >/dev/null 2>&1 || damp_should_send "$firedBy" "stranded:$pane"; then
      "$NOTIFY_BIN" "$firedBy" "⚠️ STRANDED WORK — your fired peer ${name} is ${cause} and NEVER LANDED: ${ahead} commit(s) on branch ${br:-<unknown>} are not on ${TRUNK}${dirty:+, tracked tree dirty=${dirty}}. It is done; its work is visible to nobody but this machine. Its worktree is still on disk at ${cwd} — land it from there, or confirm the work is disposable. Inspect: ${inspect_cmd}" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
      log "stranded originator-paged $firedBy for $pane ahead=$ahead dirty=$dirty branch=${br:-?}"
    else
      log "stranded originator page damped [$firedBy:$pane]"
    fi
  else
    log "stranded $pane has no firedBy originator — desk + board only (ahead=$ahead dirty=$dirty)"
  fi
  # 2. desk page. FINGERPRINT CARRIES NO COUNTS: ${ahead}/${dirty} change as a tree is worked on, and
  #    a fingerprint that moves every sweep silently defeats damping — the same trap handle_surface
  #    documents for ${detail}. State words only: this pane, stranded.
  notify_desk "⚠️ REAPER SURFACE — ${name} is ${cause} but its work NEVER LANDED: ${ahead} commit(s) on ${br:-<unknown>} not on ${TRUNK}, tracked-dirty=${dirty}, idle ${idle}s. Originator ${firedBy:-<unknown>} notified. Worktree still on disk: ${cwd}. Inspect: ${inspect_cmd}. cc-reaper never auto-acts on this — it has already DEFERRED the reap so nothing is destroyed." \
              "stranded:${name}:${pane}" || true
  local dnote
  case "${NOTIFY_OUTCOME:-unknown}" in
    reached)  dnote="desk" ;;
    recorded) dnote="desk MAILBOX (no live desk; verdict=$NOTIFY_VERDICT)" ;;
    damped)   dnote="desk (page damped inside the TTL)" ;;
    refused)  dnote="desk NOT reached (transport refused)" ;;
    *)        dnote="no desk channel wired" ;;
  esac
  # 3. blockers-board row — the one-glance operator view, and the only durable record of the finding.
  idl_stranded "$pane" "$name" "$cause" "$cwd" "$br" "$ahead" "$dirty" "$firedBy"
  say "  STRANDED $name [$cause] — ${ahead} unlanded commit(s) on ${br:-?}, tracked-dirty=${dirty} → originator ${firedBy:-<none>} + ${dnote} + board (reap still DEFERRED)"
  log "stranded surfaced $name pane=$pane cause=$cause ahead=$ahead dirty=$dirty branch=${br:-?} firedBy=${firedBy:-none} desk=${NOTIFY_OUTCOME:-unknown}"
  return 0
}

# ── P0-12b: enumerated≈live-panes self-check. `enum` = what cc-classify saw this sweep; `live` = the
#    independent interactive-pane count. A positive delta past tolerance, PERSISTED, pages the desk once. ──
self_check() { # <reap> <enumerated>
  local reap="$1" enum="$2" live delta sf
  live="$(live_pane_count)"; delta=$(( live - enum ))
  sf="$PAGEDIR/selfcheck.state"
  if [ "$delta" -le "$PANE_DELTA_TOL" ]; then
    say "cc-reaper self-check: $live live pane(s) ≈ $enum enumerated (Δ$delta ≤ tol $PANE_DELTA_TOL) — reaper sees all live panes"
    [ "$reap" = 1 ] && { mkdir -p "$PAGEDIR" 2>/dev/null || true; printf '0 0\n' > "$sf" 2>/dev/null || true; }   # re-arm
    return 0
  fi
  if [ "$reap" = 0 ]; then
    say "cc-reaper self-check: WOULD-PAGE — $live live panes vs $enum enumerated (Δ$delta unseen; a spawn mode isn't registering)"; return 0
  fi
  mkdir -p "$PAGEDIR" 2>/dev/null || true
  local consec paged
  read -r consec paged < <(cat "$sf" 2>/dev/null || echo "0 0"); consec="${consec:-0}"; paged="${paged:-0}"
  consec=$(( consec + 1 ))
  if [ "$consec" -ge "$SELFCHECK_MIN_PERSIST" ] && { [ "$paged" = 0 ] || [ "$delta" -gt "$paged" ]; }; then
    notify_desk "⚠️ REAPER SELF-CHECK — ${live} live Claude panes but only ${enum} enumerated (Δ${delta} unseen). The reaper is BLIND to ${delta} live session(s): a spawn mode isn't registering in cc-registry, so they can't be classified or reaped. Investigate: compare \`cc-sessions --json\` against the live interactive-claude procs." \
                "selfcheck-blind:delta${delta}" || true
    idl_selfcheck "$live" "$enum" "$delta"
    # Same three-way accounting as handle_surface: the blind-spot FINDING is recorded either way, but
    # only a verdict=delivered page may claim a desk saw it. `paged` (the re-page hysteresis) advances
    # in every case — the record stands, and re-paging a desk-less fleet each sweep is the storm.
    case "${NOTIFY_OUTCOME:-unknown}" in
      reached)
        say "cc-reaper self-check: PAGE — Δ$delta blind spot ($live live vs $enum enumerated) → desk"
        log "self-check page: live=$live enum=$enum delta=$delta (persisted ${consec} sweep(s))" ;;
      refused)
        say "cc-reaper self-check: Δ$delta blind spot ($live live vs $enum enumerated) — desk page REFUSED by the transport"
        log "self-check page REFUSED: live=$live enum=$enum delta=$delta (persisted ${consec} sweep(s))" ;;
      *)
        say "cc-reaper self-check: RECORD — Δ$delta blind spot ($live live vs $enum enumerated) — recorded; NO live desk received it (${NOTIFY_OUTCOME:-unknown}${NOTIFY_VERDICT:+, verdict=$NOTIFY_VERDICT})"
        log "self-check RECORDED: live=$live enum=$enum delta=$delta outcome=${NOTIFY_OUTCOME:-unknown} verdict=${NOTIFY_VERDICT:-none} (persisted ${consec} sweep(s))" ;;
    esac
    paged="$delta"
  else
    say "cc-reaper self-check: Δ$delta blind spot ($live live vs $enum enumerated) — persist ${consec}/${SELFCHECK_MIN_PERSIST}$([ "$paged" != 0 ] && printf ' (already paged; damped)')"
  fi
  printf '%s %s\n' "$consec" "$paged" > "$sf" 2>/dev/null || true
}

sweep() {
  local reap=0; [ "${1:-}" = "--reap" ] && reap=1
  local mode; mode=$([ $reap = 1 ] && echo REAP || echo DRY-RUN)
  say "cc-reaper sweep ($mode) — settle=${SETTLE_S}s trunk=$TRUNK"
  log "sweep start mode=$mode settle=$SETTLE_S"
  # Gap-3 suspend-guard: capture the sweep-start clock + the gap since the last sweep's end. A large
  # gap ⇒ the machine slept BETWEEN sweeps; a large start→act span (checked per-candidate below) ⇒ it
  # slept DURING this sweep. Either poisons the classify idle values → no reaps this sweep.
  local sweep_t0 last_beat inter_gap
  sweep_t0="$(reaper_now)"; case "$sweep_t0" in ''|*[!0-9]*) sweep_t0="$(date +%s)" ;; esac
  last_beat="$(cat "$BEAT_FILE" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)"; case "$last_beat" in ''|*[!0-9]*) last_beat=0 ;; esac
  inter_gap=0; [ "$last_beat" -gt 0 ] && inter_gap=$(( sweep_t0 - last_beat ))
  # Garbage FIRST — before reconcile and classify, because the garbage is what starves their
  # bounds: on 2026-08-07 (load 781) every sweep read "bound-fired classify … NO candidates
  # (fail-closed)" while 959 orphaned `ps` chewed ~288% CPU. Reaping the residue is cheap and
  # makes the expensive phases fit their bands again. Same reap/dry-run gate as everything else.
  garbage_sweep "$reap" || true
  # Self-heal the registry BEFORE enumerating, so a pane that missed SessionStart is backfilled and
  # ENUMERATED this same sweep (delta closes) rather than only surfaced. Additive + idempotent; gated on
  # --reap so a DRY-RUN sweep still writes nothing (its self-check then reports the true pre-heal delta).
  # R5: bounded. cc-reconcile is the ONE foreign call that must stay ahead of classify — it backfills
  # rows classify then enumerates, so deferring it would delay the delta-close by a full sweep. It is
  # therefore bounded rather than moved. (cc-backlog + cc-inbox-guard have no such ordering tie and
  # run AFTER the decision loop — see the ride-along block below.)
  if [ "$reap" = 1 ] && [ -n "$RECONCILE_BIN" ]; then
    local rout rrc
    rout="$(_rp_bounded "${CC_REAPER_RECONCILE_TIMEOUT_S:-60}" "$RECONCILE_BIN" 2>/dev/null)"; rrc=$?
    [ "$rrc" = 124 ] && log "bound-fired reconcile: exceeded ${CC_REAPER_RECONCILE_TIMEOUT_S:-60}s — registry backfill INCOMPLETE this sweep"
    # R5d (2026-08-25, recycle #225): _rp_bounded's own contract (see its comment above) is that rc 124
    # "MUST stay distinguishable from the child's own failure". R5b honoured that at the classify call
    # and NOWHERE ELSE — this fork, and the two ride-alongs below, branched on 124 alone, so a child
    # that died of its own accord left NO line at all: rc discarded, stderr sent to /dev/null, and the
    # summary `say` below skipped because a dead producer printed nothing. Measured over the log's
    # 1,662 completed REAP sweeps, this arm has fired ZERO times, so it is LATENT, not active — but it
    # is reachable (cc-inbox-guard:95 exits 2 when hooks/lib/mailbox-pending.sh is unusable, and the
    # live layer already carries 3 absent files under hooks/), and an arm that cannot speak cannot be
    # measured either: a census of a store whose producer has no emitting line is a NON-VERDICT.
    [ "$rrc" != 0 ] && [ "$rrc" != 124 ] && log "reconcile FAILED: rc=$rrc — producer error, NOT the bound; registry backfill did NOT run this sweep"
    [ -n "$rout" ] && { say "$rout"; log "reconcile: $rout"; }
  fi
  # ── THE DECISION begins here. classify_t0 is the instant the evidence every reap rests on was
  #    taken; A8's "decision→act gap" is measured from THIS stamp, not from sweep_t0 (which precedes
  #    it by whatever the reconcile call cost, i.e. time that elapsed BEFORE the evidence existed and
  #    so cannot be part of its age). sweep_t0 is left untouched — the Gap-3 suspend guard below is
  #    calibrated to it, and both spans are logged so neither number is hidden by the other.
  local cj crc classify_t0
  cj="$(_rp_bounded "${CC_REAPER_CLASSIFY_TIMEOUT_S:-90}" "$CLASSIFY_BIN" --all --json 2>/dev/null)"; crc=$?
  if [ "$crc" = 124 ]; then
    # BOUND-FIT-THE-BAND (2026-08-07): 90s was sized on a healthy box. Under exactly the overload
    # a sweep exists to relieve, a fixed bound is a PERMANENT non-verdict — every sweep of the
    # load-781 incident logged this line while 153 panes accumulated, so the mechanism designed to
    # relieve pressure was disabled BY the pressure. One retry at 3× keeps the fail-closed
    # contract (still never a partial verdict) while giving the producer a band-fitted budget.
    log "bound-fired classify: exceeded ${CC_REAPER_CLASSIFY_TIMEOUT_S:-90}s — retrying once at 3x"
    cj="$(_rp_bounded $(( ${CC_REAPER_CLASSIFY_TIMEOUT_S:-90} * 3 )) "$CLASSIFY_BIN" --all --json 2>/dev/null)"; crc=$?
  fi
  if [ "$crc" = 124 ]; then
    # The evidence producer itself wedged, twice. There is no verdict this sweep, so there is nothing
    # to act on — a bounded classify must yield an EMPTY candidate set, never a partial one a reap
    # could ride.
    say "cc-classify exceeded $(( ${CC_REAPER_CLASSIFY_TIMEOUT_S:-90} * 3 ))s even on retry — no verdict this sweep; reaping nothing"
    log "bound-fired classify: retry exceeded $(( ${CC_REAPER_CLASSIFY_TIMEOUT_S:-90} * 3 ))s — sweep yields NO candidates (fail-closed)"
    cj='[]'
  fi
  if [ "$crc" != 0 ] && [ "$crc" != 124 ]; then
    # R5b (2026-08-25, recycle #220): the BOUND was not the only way this producer fails, and until
    # now it was the only way that got NAMED. rc 124 is timeout(1)'s; any other nonzero rc is the
    # child's own failure — a missing binary (127), a crash, a shell error — and it fell straight
    # through the `case "$cj" in '')` line below to an empty candidate set, printing "0 classified"
    # byte-identically to a healthy idle sweep. _rp_bounded's own comment requires rc 124 to "stay
    # distinguishable from the child's own failure"; it defended only one direction of that.
    say "cc-classify FAILED rc=$crc (not a timeout) — no verdict this sweep; reaping nothing"
    log "classify FAILED: rc=$crc — producer error, NOT the bound; sweep yields NO candidates (fail-closed)"
    cj='[]'
  fi
  case "$cj" in '') cj='[]' ;; esac
  classify_t0="$(reaper_now)"; case "$classify_t0" in ''|*[!0-9]*) classify_t0="$(date +%s)" ;; esac
  local n; n="$(printf '%s' "$cj" | jq 'length' 2>/dev/null || echo 0)"
  # R5c (2026-08-25, recycle #220): AN EMPTY CLASSIFICATION IS ONLY CREDIBLE IF THE BOX IS EMPTY, and
  # no rc arm above can establish that. cc-classify:951 reads
  #   all="$("$SESSIONS_BIN" --json 2>/dev/null || echo '[]')"
  # and cmd_all then `return 0` — so a dead, missing or garbage-emitting enumerator yields an EMPTY
  # ARRAY AT rc 0. Measured three arms against the real binary: healthy = rc 0 / 8 entries; enumerator
  # exits 3 = rc 0 / [] / zero stderr; enumerator emits non-JSON at rc 0 = rc 0 / []. The healthy run
  # and the failed one are INDISTINGUISHABLE BY rc, so widening the rc branch — the remedy the backlog
  # row prescribes — has zero power over the mode that actually fires here. The signal is the LENGTH,
  # and length is only readable against an INDEPENDENT count of live panes, which this file already
  # owns (live_pane_count, deliberately not registry-derived — see its header).
  # Over the 5,424 summarised sweeps in cc-reaper.out.log, 1,145 ended "0 classified" and ALL 1,145
  # had live>0 (median 17, max 54): a legitimately-empty classification has never once been observed.
  # This changes the RECORD, never the action — n is already 0, so nothing was going to be reaped
  # either way. Being blind and saying so nowhere IS the defect.
  #
  # SCOPED TO THE SUCCEEDING PRODUCER (2026-08-25, recycle #224) — R5c above rejected "widen the rc
  # branch" as having zero power over the mode that actually fires, and it was right; but the guard
  # it built instead was gated on `n == 0` ALONE, which is satisfied by every path that reaches here,
  # including the two directly above that have ALREADY named their own cause. Both of those set
  # cj='[]' before falling through, so R5c re-diagnosed them — and named a DIFFERENT and WRONG cause:
  #     bound-fired classify: retry exceeded 3s — sweep yields NO candidates (fail-closed)
  #     classify EMPTY-BUT-POPULATED: 0 enumerated vs 4 live pane(s) — enumerator failed open at rc=124
  # rc=124 is timeout(1)'s "the bound fired" code, so the second line contradicts the field it prints
  # in its own text, and the say() beside it tells the operator the enumerator "failed open" when the
  # enumerator was never given a chance to answer — it was killed. #220's two R5c tests both pin
  # rc 0 (empty/live>0 and empty/live==0); neither exercises a nonzero crc, which is why a predicate
  # this wide passed. Reproduced by execution before fixing, not inferred from reading.
  # SCALE, and why it read clean: this has fired ZERO times in the log's 1,673 sweeps because R5c is
  # landed-not-live (~/.claude/bin/cc-reaper is a symlink into the shared checkout, 58 commits behind;
  # EMPTY-BUT-POPULATED count is 0 there and 1 on trunk). It is LATENT, and it arrives in full the
  # moment the live layer converges: 289 of those sweeps (17.3%) took the rc-124 arm with live panes
  # present, so each would have carried the wrong subsystem's name exactly once.
  # The signal R5c exists for is unchanged — an enumerator that fails OPEN returns rc 0, so scoping to
  # crc==0 removes nothing it can detect and only stops it answering questions already answered.
  if [ "$n" = 0 ] && [ "$crc" = 0 ]; then
    local live_now; live_now="$(live_pane_count 2>/dev/null)"
    case "$live_now" in ''|*[!0-9]*) live_now=0 ;; esac
    if [ "$live_now" -gt 0 ]; then
      say "cc-classify returned an EMPTY classification while $live_now live pane(s) exist — NO VERDICT this sweep (the enumerator failed open); reaping nothing"
      log "classify EMPTY-BUT-POPULATED: 0 enumerated vs $live_now live pane(s) — enumerator failed open at rc=$crc; sweep yields NO candidates"
    fi
  fi
  local reaped=0 candidates=0 surfaced=0 surfaced_keys=" " i
  for ((i=0; i<n; i++)); do
    local o cause name pane cwd idle landed xpid xlstart started fstate
    o="$(printf '%s' "$cj" | jq -c ".[$i]")"
    cause="$(printf '%s' "$o" | jq -r '.cause // "?"')"
    name="$(printf '%s'  "$o" | jq -r '.name // .paneUUID // "?"')"
    pane="$(printf '%s'  "$o" | jq -r '.paneUUID // ""')"
    cwd="$(printf '%s'   "$o" | jq -r '.cwd // ""')"
    idle="$(printf '%s'  "$o" | jq -r '.idle_s // -1')"
    landed="$(printf '%s' "$o" | jq -r '.work_landed // "no"')"
    xpid="$(printf '%s'  "$o" | jq -r '.pid // ""')"        # classify-time identity pin (a17 S-4)
    xlstart="$(printf '%s' "$o" | jq -r '.lstart // ""')"
    started="$(printf '%s' "$o" | jq -r '.startedAt // ""')"   # epoch-MS; stamp-tenancy input (rule 2)

    # ── stamp tenancy (2026-07-24 rule 2): a fired stamp gates a pane's CURRENT session only if that
    #    session booted within FIRED_BOOT_MAX_S of the fire. A stamp found STALE by this rule (a later
    #    tenant reused the pane) is GC'd HERE so the dir cannot carry a dead worker's contract forever
    #    and so both fired_peer legs below (promotion, belt) see it gone. INVALID (unknown startedAt) is
    #    NOT GC'd — it may just be a transiently-missing registry field; it only fails toward surface.
    fstate="$(fired_stamp_state "$pane" "$started")"
    if [ "$fstate" = stale ]; then
      local _fa; _fa="$(jq -r '.firedAt // ""' "$FIRED_DIR/$pane.json" 2>/dev/null || true)"
      rm -f "$FIRED_DIR/$pane.json" 2>/dev/null || true
      say "  gc     $name — stale-tenancy fired stamp dropped (tenant booted > ${FIRED_BOOT_MAX_S}s after fire)"
      log "stale-tenancy stamp GC pane=$pane firedAt=${_fa} startedAt=${started}"
    fi

    # ── T-P3-4 PROMOTION: a desk-fired peer worker that is finished + landed + tracked-clean is
    #    auto-reaped instead of paged for confirm-close. THREE independent gates, all required:
    #    (1) the cause is promotable (finished-shared-review only — see AUTOREAP_FIRED_RE),
    #    (2) the SPAWNER marked this pane a self-retiring fired peer (absence ⇒ operator ⇒ never), and
    #    (3) work_landed re-verifies HERE, at act time, that its OWN TRACKED tree is clean AND the
    #        content is on trunk. Untracked litter (usually a live sibling's) no longer blocks this;
    #        TRACKED dirt still does, and falls through to the surface path exactly as before.
    #    A promoted session then faces every ORIGINAL gate too: settle window, checkpoint-first,
    #    the post-classify work_landed re-check, and cc-teardown's own independent gate.
    local promoted=0
    if [ "$AUTOREAP_FIRED" = 1 ] && [[ "$cause" =~ $AUTOREAP_FIRED_RE ]] && fired_peer "$pane" "$started"; then
      if work_landed "$cwd"; then
        promoted=1; landed=yes
        say "  promote $name [$cause] — desk-fired peer, own TRACKED tree clean + landed → auto-reap (no confirm-close)"
        log "promote $name [$cause] pane=$pane: fired peer, tracked-clean + landed → reapable"
      else
        say "  keep   $name [$cause] — desk-fired peer but TRACKED tree dirty / not landed → surface (never auto-reap)"
      fi
    fi

    # ── safeguard-blocked: a fired peer refused by model content-safeguards on arrival. A DEDICATED
    #    surface (originator page + board row + desk page + opt-in auto-recover), intercepted BEFORE the
    #    generic desk-only surface path. NEVER reaped.
    if [ "$promoted" = 0 ] && [ "$cause" = safeguard-blocked ]; then
      surfaced=$((surfaced+1))
      local sgkey; sgkey="$(sanitize_key "${pane:-$name}")"
      surfaced_keys="$surfaced_keys$sgkey "
      handle_safeguard_blocked "$reap" "$o"
      continue
    fi

    # ── disposition (never-reap is the default; a reap needs every gate) ──
    if [ "$promoted" = 0 ] && ! [[ "$cause" =~ $REAPABLE_RE ]]; then
      # T-P3-3: a never-reap cause that needs a HUMAN → page the desk (damped); the rest just stay quiet.
      if [[ "$cause" =~ $SURFACE_PAGE_RE ]]; then
        surfaced=$((surfaced+1))
        local key; key="$(sanitize_key "${pane:-$name}")"
        surfaced_keys="$surfaced_keys$key "
        handle_surface "$reap" "$key" "$name" "$cause" "$pane" "$(printf '%s' "$o" | jq -r '.detail // ""')"
        # …and if that session ALSO died holding unlanded work, say so to the ORIGINATOR. handle_surface
        # is desk-only and names no branch, no count and no remedy that fits — a crash is the canonical
        # "no close path ever ran", which is the exact case backlog 1b19ab3096d2 leg 2 is about, so
        # without this the row's CENTRAL case reaches nobody who could act on it. The two pages are
        # different findings for different audiences ("this pane crashed" vs "its work is not on
        # trunk") and damp independently.
        [[ "$cause" =~ $STRANDED_SURFACE_RE ]] && handle_stranded "$reap" "$name" "$cause" "$pane" "$cwd" "$idle"
      else
        say "  keep   $name [$cause] — never-reap cause"
      fi
      continue
    fi
    if [ "$landed" != yes ]; then
      say "  keep   $name [$cause] — work NOT landed (would strand work) → DEFER"; log "defer $name: not landed"
      # The DEFER above is unchanged and still the whole safety story — nothing is destroyed. What was
      # missing is that it told NOBODY: this reaper is the last actor that can see the branch of a
      # session which never ran a close path. handle_stranded re-derives the evidence at act time and
      # routes it to the originator; it never reaps (backlog 1b19ab3096d2 leg 2).
      handle_stranded "$reap" "$name" "$cause" "$pane" "$cwd" "$idle"
      continue
    fi
    if ! [ "$idle" -ge "$SETTLE_S" ] 2>/dev/null; then
      say "  keep   $name [$cause] — idle ${idle}s < settle ${SETTLE_S}s (self-close still has its chance)"; continue
    fi
    # ── 2026-07-24 belt (Danny-Studio-60 / Opus-5 incident): finished/finished-teammate may only
    #    auto-reap a SPAWNER-STAMPED fired peer whose stamp is TENANCY-VALID (rule 2 — a later tenant's
    #    stale stamp was GC'd above and reads unstamped here). An unstamped/stale pane is operator-
    #    launched/adopted (or predates the marker) — surface it for confirm-close instead, even if a
    #    stale or foreign classifier labeled it reapable (cc-classify enforces the same gate; this is the
    #    reaper's own independent leg). handed-off-lead (live firedBy-stamped successor) and
    #    coordination-abandoned (dead-partner horizon + live co-cwd owner) rest on their own positive
    #    evidence and are exempt.
    if [[ "$cause" =~ ^(finished|finished-teammate)$ ]] && ! fired_peer "$pane" "$started"; then
      say "  keep   $name [$cause] — unstamped/stale operator pane (no tenancy-valid fired-peer marker): auto-reap refused (2026-07-24 belt)"
      log "belt-refuse $name [$cause] pane=$pane: no tenancy-valid fired-peer marker"
      surfaced=$((surfaced+1))
      local bkey; bkey="$(sanitize_key "${pane:-$name}")"
      surfaced_keys="$surfaced_keys$bkey "
      handle_surface "$reap" "$bkey" "$name" "$cause" "$pane" "unstamped operator pane (no fired-peer marker) — auto-reap refused; confirm-close if truly done"
      continue
    fi
    # ── UNCOMMITTED-PEER BELT (2026-08-19, backlog 7c22e9b43956 — the B2D1CE68 death) ───────────
    # The belt directly above answers "is this pane allowed to be auto-reaped at all?". This one
    # answers the question the `[ "$landed" != yes ]` DEFER guard only LOOKS like it answers: "is
    # there positive evidence this session finished?" For a session that never committed, `ahead=0`
    # is silence on the git channel, so the only channel left is the one the fire contract armed —
    # its completion ping. Both silent ⇒ nothing has said this session is done, and the reap would
    # rest on inference. SURFACE it (the pane lives, the desk is paged, damped) rather than reap.
    #
    # NARROWED TO WHERE A PING WAS ACTUALLY PROMISED. A peer fired WITHOUT --notify-back never owed
    # an announcement, so its silence carries no information and this belt must stay quiet over it —
    # the same polarity rule sc_announce_before_retire applies to itself, and what keeps the belt's
    # firing rate a signal instead of a tax. Measured before wiring: 144 of 938 fired stamps on this
    # box carry notifyBack, and of 30 recently-reaped panes 14 had a send record and 16 did not, so
    # this reads BOTH ways on the live population rather than always-on or always-off.
    #
    # THREE-VALUED THROUGHOUT, AND ONLY THE DEFINITE NEGATIVES COMBINE. Anything either oracle
    # cannot answer proceeds exactly as before, loudly enough to be found in the log.
    #
    # ── THE CAUSE SET HAD TO MATCH THE DOOR, NOT THE LABEL (2026-08-20, same row) ────────────────
    # This gate read `cause =~ ^(finished|finished-teammate)$` for its first day, which is the cause
    # set of ONE of the two doors into the reap path. A DISPATCHED PEER — the thing the row's title
    # names — does not come through that door at all: cc-classify labels a desk-fired peer
    # `finished-shared-review`, and it reaches the reap path via the T-P3-4 PROMOTION above, which
    # sets promoted=1 and thereby bypasses REAPABLE_RE at the disposition gate. The belt never
    # consulted $promoted, so all four legs were skipped for exactly the population they were built
    # to protect, and `work_landed`'s 0-ahead-by-silence fast path was load-bearing there with
    # nothing behind it.
    #
    # MEASURED, not reasoned: over the 7 days to 2026-08-20 the live reaper log carries 467
    # `[finished-shared-review]` sightings against 71 `[finished-teammate]`, and the other two
    # REAPABLE_RE causes (`finished`, `coordination-abandoned`) were emitted ZERO times. The belt
    # shipped measured against the minority door. Reproduced end-to-end on the majority one: a
    # 0-commit, never-announced promoted peer read `1 reaped`, cc-teardown was invoked and the
    # worktree was REMOVED (tests/cc-reaper.bats B11, red pre-fix).
    #
    # STRICTLY SAFETY-INCREASING, which is why it is a widening and not a re-scope: every leg below
    # can only refuse-and-continue or fall through, so admitting a population can convert a reap
    # into a surface and can never do the reverse. B12 is the discriminator that stops it degrading
    # into "never auto-reap a desk-fired peer" — a promoted peer that DID commit still reaps, so the
    # confirm-close pile-up T-P3-4 exists to drain stays drained.
    if [ "$UNCOMMITTED_BELT" = 1 ] && { [[ "$cause" =~ ^(finished|finished-teammate)$ ]] || [ "$promoted" = 1 ]; }; then
      local ub_nb ub_c=0 ub_p=0 ub_i=0 ub_sid
      # HOISTED (2026-08-19b): never-committed is the precondition BOTH legs share, so it is asked
      # once, ahead of either. This changes no verdict — leg 2 required it already — it just makes
      # the answer available to leg 3, whose population is exactly the peers leg 2 must stay quiet
      # over (no back-channel armed ⇒ no announcement was ever owed).
      session_ever_committed "$cwd" || ub_c=$?
      if [ "$ub_c" = 1 ]; then
        # ── LEG 3: an Agent-tool subagent of this session is still IN FLIGHT ────────────────────
        # POSITIVE evidence, so it outranks leg 2's inferred-silence read and is tried first, and it
        # applies whether or not a back-channel was ever armed. Reaping here SIGKILLs a running
        # subagent mid-work; nothing downstream ever learns it existed.
        if [ "$INFLIGHT_BELT" = 1 ]; then
          ub_sid="$(printf '%s' "$o" | jq -r '.session_id // .sessionId // ""' 2>/dev/null)"
          session_subagent_inflight "$ub_sid" || ub_i=$?
          case "$ub_i" in
            0)
              say "  keep   $name [$cause] — NEVER COMMITTED here and an Agent-tool subagent is still IN FLIGHT: a reap would SIGKILL it mid-run → auto-reap refused (in-flight-subagent belt)"
              log "inflight-belt refuse $name [$cause] pane=$pane cwd=$cwd sid=${ub_sid:-none}: 0 commits in this worktree's reflog AND a live subagent transcript newer than the session's own, silent < ${INFLIGHT_MAX_SILENCE_S}s — reap would kill in-flight work"
              surfaced=$((surfaced+1))
              local ibkey; ibkey="$(sanitize_key "${pane:-$name}")"
              surfaced_keys="$surfaced_keys$ibkey "
              handle_surface "$reap" "$ibkey" "$name" "$cause" "$pane" "never committed in this worktree AND an Agent-tool subagent is still running — auto-reap refused; closing this pane kills it mid-run. Confirm-close if truly done"
              continue ;;
            2) log "inflight-belt abstain $name pane=$pane: cannot resolve this session's transcript for sid=${ub_sid:-none} — proceeding to the back-channel leg" ;;
          esac
        fi
        # ── LEG 4: the peer's OWN contract is still UNMET (backlog 7c22e9b43956) ────────────────
        # POSITIVE evidence like leg 3, so it sits with it above leg 2's inferred-silence read, and
        # like leg 3 it applies whether or not a back-channel was ever armed — which is the entire
        # point: leg 2 is structurally unable to reach the 83% of fired peers that carry no
        # notifyBack, and this is the leg that reaches the goal-armed SLICE of them — measured at
        # ~1% of that hole (2 of 344), never the hole itself; see the header. Ordered AFTER leg 3
        # because leg 3's refusal is
        # the more concrete destruction (a reap SIGKILLs a subagent mid-run), so it should own the
        # message when both are true.
        if [ "$GOAL_BELT" = 1 ]; then
          local ub_g=0
          [ -n "${ub_sid:-}" ] || ub_sid="$(printf '%s' "$o" | jq -r '.session_id // .sessionId // ""' 2>/dev/null)"
          session_goal_inflight "$ub_sid" || ub_g=$?
          case "$ub_g" in
            0)
              say "  keep   $name [$cause] — NEVER COMMITTED here and its own /goal is still LIVE and UNMET: the session's own record says it is not done → auto-reap refused (goal-contract belt)"
              log "goal-belt refuse $name [$cause] pane=$pane cwd=$cwd sid=${ub_sid:-none}: 0 commits in this worktree's reflog AND the last goal_status in its transcript is neither met nor failed — reap would destroy work that lives only in the transcript"
              surfaced=$((surfaced+1))
              local gbkey; gbkey="$(sanitize_key "${pane:-$name}")"
              surfaced_keys="$surfaced_keys$gbkey "
              handle_surface "$reap" "$gbkey" "$name" "$cause" "$pane" "never committed in this worktree AND its own /goal is still unmet — auto-reap refused; its work (if any) is in its transcript only. Confirm-close if truly done"
              continue ;;
            1) log "goal-belt pass $name pane=$pane: 0 commits and no LIVE unmet goal (never armed one, or it is met/failed) — not evidence of in-flight work, proceeding to the back-channel leg" ;;
            2) log "goal-belt abstain $name pane=$pane: cannot resolve a goal verdict for sid=${ub_sid:-none} (lib/jq/transcript unavailable) — NOT treating an unresolved read as a negative, proceeding to the back-channel leg" ;;
          esac
        fi
        # ── LEG 2 (2026-08-19a): a back-channel WAS armed and was never used ────────────────────
        ub_nb="$(peer_notify_back "$pane")"
        if [ -n "$ub_nb" ]; then
          peer_ping_verdict "$pane" "$ub_nb" || ub_p=$?
          case "$ub_p" in
            1)
              say "  keep   $name [$cause] — NEVER COMMITTED here and never announced to its armed back-channel ($ub_nb): 'landed' is 0-ahead-by-silence, not done-evidence → auto-reap refused (uncommitted-peer belt)"
              log "uncommitted-belt refuse $name [$cause] pane=$pane cwd=$cwd notifyBack=$ub_nb: 0 commits in this worktree's reflog AND no send record — reap would be inferred from silence"
              surfaced=$((surfaced+1))
              local ubkey; ubkey="$(sanitize_key "${pane:-$name}")"
              surfaced_keys="$surfaced_keys$ubkey "
              handle_surface "$reap" "$ubkey" "$name" "$cause" "$pane" "never committed in this worktree AND never pinged $ub_nb — auto-reap refused; its work (if any) is in its transcript only. Confirm-close if truly done"
              continue ;;
            0) log "uncommitted-belt pass $name pane=$pane: 0 commits but DID announce to $ub_nb — positive done-evidence, reap allowed" ;;
            *) log "uncommitted-belt abstain $name pane=$pane: 0 commits, ping verdict UNKNOWN for $ub_nb (no store / legacy or unreadable record) — proceeding as before, NOT treating unknown as silence" ;;
          esac
        fi
      elif [ "$ub_c" = 2 ]; then
        # Attached to the never-committed branch, NOT to the back-channel one: after the hoist above
        # `ub_c = 2` is unreachable inside `ub_c = 1`, so an elif left down there would be dead code
        # that silently deletes this abstain line — the one record that says the belt looked.
        log "uncommitted-belt abstain $name pane=$pane: cannot tell whether this cwd ever committed ($cwd) — proceeding as before"
      fi
    fi
    # ── Gap-2 second legs (2026-07-25): the belt above is the independent leg for finished/
    #    finished-teammate. coordination-abandoned and handed-off-lead had NONE — they rested on
    #    cc-classify's decision alone (coordination-abandoned specifically on the §4.7 operator hold
    #    being ordered before its branch: load-bearing + undefended). Each gets an independent
    #    act-time leg here. Both FAIL CLOSED: any ambiguity ⇒ surface, never reap.
    if [ "$cause" = coordination-abandoned ]; then
      # Independent operator-adoption re-check via ce_last_interactive_age — a SEPARATE implementation
      # from the classifier's §4.7. Normal-case NO-OP (a coordination-abandoned session already cleared
      # §4.7, so its last operator prompt is ≥ hold or absent); it BITES only when §4.7 was bypassed/
      # reordered and a live operator conversation reached this branch. Reads WHO drove the last turn.
      local cah_sid cah_tj cah_age cah_key
      cah_sid="$(printf '%s' "$o" | jq -r '.session_id // .sessionId // ""')"
      cah_tj="$(find_transcript "$cah_sid" 2>/dev/null || true)"
      cah_key="$(sanitize_key "${pane:-$name}")"
      if [ -z "$cah_tj" ] || ! command -v ce_last_interactive_age >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        say "  keep   $name [$cause] — cannot prove no operator adoption (transcript/lib unresolvable) → surface (fail-closed)"
        log "belt-refuse $name [$cause] pane=$pane: transcript/lib unresolvable (fail-closed)"
        surfaced=$((surfaced+1)); surfaced_keys="$surfaced_keys$cah_key "
        handle_surface "$reap" "$cah_key" "$name" "$cause" "$pane" "cannot prove no operator adoption (transcript/lib unresolvable) — auto-reap refused; confirm-close if truly abandoned"
        continue
      fi
      cah_age="$(ce_last_interactive_age "$cah_tj" 2>/dev/null || true)"
      # 'unreadable' (the 2026-07-25 empty-answer split): the transcript RESOLVED but could not be read
      # (corrupt / truncated / empty / no jq) — absence of evidence, not "nobody typed". Same fail-closed
      # disposition as the unresolvable case above; before the split this fell through to reap.
      if [ "$cah_age" = unreadable ]; then
        say "  keep   $name [$cause] — cannot prove no operator adoption (transcript unreadable) → surface (fail-closed)"
        log "belt-refuse $name [$cause] pane=$pane: transcript unreadable (fail-closed)"
        surfaced=$((surfaced+1)); surfaced_keys="$surfaced_keys$cah_key "
        handle_surface "$reap" "$cah_key" "$name" "$cause" "$pane" "cannot prove no operator adoption (transcript unreadable) — auto-reap refused; confirm-close if truly abandoned"
        continue
      fi
      if [ -n "$cah_age" ] && [ "$cah_age" -lt "$INTERACTIVE_HOLD_S" ] 2>/dev/null; then
        say "  keep   $name [$cause] — operator prompt ${cah_age}s ago (< hold ${INTERACTIVE_HOLD_S}s): operator-adopted, auto-reap refused (Gap-2 leg)"
        log "belt-refuse $name [$cause] pane=$pane: operator prompt ${cah_age}s ago < hold ${INTERACTIVE_HOLD_S}s"
        surfaced=$((surfaced+1)); surfaced_keys="$surfaced_keys$cah_key "
        handle_surface "$reap" "$cah_key" "$name" "$cause" "$pane" "operator prompt ${cah_age}s ago (< interactive hold) — operator-adopted; auto-reap refused"
        continue
      fi
    fi
    if [ "$cause" = handed-off-lead ]; then
      # Independent positive-evidence re-check: the LIVE successor that justifies reaping the
      # predecessor must STILL be alive AT ACT TIME (it can die between classify and act). Look it up
      # in the SAME classify output the reaper already holds; no live successor ⇒ continuity unproven.
      local hol_succ hol_pid hol_key
      hol_succ="$(printf '%s' "$o" | jq -r '.successor // ""')"
      hol_pid="$(printf '%s' "$cj" | jq -r --arg s "$hol_succ" 'if $s=="" then empty else (.[] | select(.paneUUID==$s) | .pid) end' 2>/dev/null | head -1)"
      if [ -z "$hol_succ" ] || [ -z "$hol_pid" ] || [ "$hol_pid" = null ] || ! alive "$hol_pid"; then
        hol_key="$(sanitize_key "${pane:-$name}")"
        say "  keep   $name [$cause] — successor ${hol_succ:-none} not live at act time: handoff continuity unproven, auto-reap refused (Gap-2 leg)"
        log "belt-refuse $name [$cause] pane=$pane: successor ${hol_succ:-none} pid=${hol_pid:-none} not alive at act time"
        surfaced=$((surfaced+1)); surfaced_keys="$surfaced_keys$hol_key "
        handle_surface "$reap" "$hol_key" "$name" "$cause" "$pane" "handed-off successor not live at act time — continuity unproven; confirm-close if truly handed off"
        continue
      fi
    fi
    # ── Gap-3 suspend-guard: reap NOTHING on a sweep whose wall-clock crossed a machine SUSPEND. Two
    #    detectors — inter-sweep gap (slept BETWEEN sweeps) + intra-sweep span (slept DURING this sweep,
    #    so classify's idle values sit on the far side of the jump). Checked HERE (after all surface/
    #    belt/leg paging above, so a suspend never suppresses a surface) and only for a would-be reap.
    local now_act intra
    now_act="$(reaper_now)"; case "$now_act" in ''|*[!0-9]*) now_act="$(date +%s)" ;; esac
    intra=$(( now_act - sweep_t0 ))
    if [ "$inter_gap" -gt "$SUSPEND_S" ] || [ "$intra" -gt "$SUSPEND_S" ]; then
      say "  keep   $name [$cause] — post-suspend cooldown (sweep spans a machine sleep: inter-gap ${inter_gap}s / intra ${intra}s > ${SUSPEND_S}s) → DEFER (never reap on stale idle)"
      log "suspend-defer $name [$cause] pane=$pane: inter_gap=${inter_gap}s intra=${intra}s > ${SUSPEND_S}s"
      continue
    fi
    candidates=$((candidates+1))
    if [ $reap = 0 ]; then
      say "  WOULD-REAP $name [$cause] pane=$pane idle=${idle}s landed=yes"; log "would-reap $name [$cause]"; continue
    fi

    # ── ACT: checkpoint-first → re-verify landed → cc-teardown (double-gate) → worktree cleanup ──
    say "  REAP   $name [$cause] pane=$pane"
    checkpoint_first "$cwd" "$name" "$(printf '%s' "$o" | jq -r '.paneUUID')"
    if ! work_landed "$cwd"; then
      say "    ABORT — became dirty/ahead post-classify; WIP checkpointed, NOT reaping"; log "abort $name: dirty post-classify (WIP checkpointed)"; continue
    fi
    local succ ev trc
    succ="$(printf '%s' "$o" | jq -r '.successor // "none"')"
    ev="cc-reaper: cause=$cause successor=$succ work-landed(clean & 0 ahead of $TRUNK) idle=${idle}s — positive done-evidence, not inferred from silence"
    # A PROMOTED reap rests on a DIFFERENT basis than the generic one, so the durable audit string
    # must say so rather than assert evidence it does not have (T-P3-4).
    [ "$promoted" = 1 ] && ev="cc-reaper: cause=$cause (T-P3-4 auto-reap) — desk-fired peer worker under the self-retire contract; own TRACKED tree clean AND content landed on $TRUNK, re-verified at act time; idle=${idle}s. Untracked litter in the shared cwd is the live sibling's, not this session's."
    # identity pin (a17 S-4): pass classify-time {pid,lstart} so cc-teardown REFUSES if the pane recycled
    # in the classify→act window (successor booted in the same pane). Only pin when classify supplied it.
    local expect_args=()
    { [ -n "$xpid" ] && [ "$xpid" != null ]; } && expect_args+=(--expect-pid "$xpid")
    { [ -n "$xlstart" ] && [ "$xlstart" != null ]; } && expect_args+=(--expect-lstart "$xlstart")
    # ── ACT-TIME EVIDENCE RE-TAKE + freshness lease (SESSION_REGISTRY_V2 §4.3, R1/R2/R3) ─────────
    # The lease is only worth the evidence behind it. Stamping `now` right before the call would
    # certify freshness the EVIDENCE does not have — the classify verdict is up to a whole sweep
    # old (p99 2,099s) — and would make the lease a rubber stamp. So the safety-critical half of
    # the verdict is RE-TAKEN here, at act time, from the O(1) beat, for EVERY reapable cause.
    # (v1 re-took it for `coordination-abandoned` only, while 95% of all reaps ever performed —
    # 76/80 — rode `finished`/`finished-teammate`, whose only independent act-time leg was a STATIC
    # marker. That asymmetry is the 2026-07-24 hole.) `decided_at` is then the timestamp OF THIS
    # READ, so the lease cc-teardown enforces is a claim about real evidence.
    local rt_sid rt_age decided_at decided_age rt_key
    rt_sid="$(printf '%s' "$o" | jq -r '.session_id // .sessionId // ""' 2>/dev/null)"
    rt_key="$(sanitize_key "${pane:-$name}")"
    if [ "${CC_REAP_BEAT_RETAKE:-on}" != off ] && type -t cb_operator_age >/dev/null 2>&1; then
      if cb_system_live 2>/dev/null; then
        # The beat world is provably producing, so a per-session answer here is trustworthy.
        rt_age="$(cb_operator_age "$rt_sid" 2>/dev/null || true)"
        if [ -z "$rt_age" ]; then
          # Beat-less SESSION inside a LIVE beat world: cannot prove the operator is absent ⇒ refuse
          # and surface (R3). Absence of evidence is never evidence of absence.
          say "  keep   $name [$cause] — no presence beat for sid=${rt_sid:-none} in a live beat world: cannot prove no operator adoption → surface (fail-closed)"
          log "beat-refuse $name [$cause] pane=$pane: no beat for sid=${rt_sid:-none} (beat system live)"
          surfaced=$((surfaced+1)); surfaced_keys="$surfaced_keys$rt_key "
          handle_surface "$reap" "$rt_key" "$name" "$cause" "$pane" "no presence beat in a live beat world — cannot prove the operator is absent; auto-reap refused"
          continue
        fi
        if [ "$rt_age" -lt "$INTERACTIVE_HOLD_S" ] 2>/dev/null; then
          say "  keep   $name [$cause] — operator beat ${rt_age}s ago (< hold ${INTERACTIVE_HOLD_S}s): operator-adopted at ACT time, auto-reap refused"
          log "beat-refuse $name [$cause] pane=$pane: operator beat ${rt_age}s ago < hold ${INTERACTIVE_HOLD_S}s"
          surfaced=$((surfaced+1)); surfaced_keys="$surfaced_keys$rt_key "
          handle_surface "$reap" "$rt_key" "$name" "$cause" "$pane" "operator prompt ${rt_age}s ago (act-time beat) — operator-adopted; auto-reap refused"
          continue
        fi
      else
        # R4 EXISTENCE GATE: nothing is beating at all ⇒ the producer is not deployed. Refusing
        # every reap here would silently inert the reaper and report a healthy 100% abstain — the
        # absence-alarm trap. So this is DETECTED and LOUD, and the sweep falls through to the
        # pre-existing v1 legs (classify §4.7 + cc-teardown's transcript belt), which still hold.
        say "  note   beat system not live (no beat younger than ${CC_BEAT_LIVE_MAX_S:-900}s) — act-time re-take unavailable; falling back to v1 legs"
        log "beat-system-inert: no fresh beat; act-time re-take skipped for $name [$cause] (producer not deployed?)"
      fi
    fi
    decided_at="$(reaper_now)"; case "$decided_at" in ''|*[!0-9]*) decided_at="$(date +%s)" ;; esac
    # A8's read is the DECISION→ACT gap: how long after the evidence was taken did we act on it. The
    # evidence is the classify verdict, so the span starts at classify_t0. It previously started at
    # sweep_t0, which sits BEFORE classify ran and so charged this gap for foreign work that finished
    # before the evidence existed — an overstatement of the thing being bounded, not a measure of it.
    # sweep_age is logged beside it so the wider span stays visible and the change is auditable.
    decided_age=$(( decided_at - classify_t0 ))
    local sweep_age=$(( decided_at - sweep_t0 ))
    "$TEARDOWN_BIN" "$pane" --done-evidence "$ev" --decided-at "$decided_at" "${expect_args[@]+"${expect_args[@]}"}"; trc=$?
    if [ "$trc" = 0 ]; then
      reaped=$((reaped+1)); say "    torn down + effect-verified (cc-teardown exit 0)"; log "reaped $name [$cause] pane=$pane decided_age=${decided_age}s sweep_age=${sweep_age}s"
      clear_fired_marker "$pane"        # the pane is gone — retire its fired-peer marker (T-P3-4)
      worktree_cleanup "$cwd"
    elif [ "$trc" = 6 ]; then
      # INDETERMINATE (cc-teardown exit 6): the process is gone but the pane's fate was UNREADABLE.
      # NOT "its gate refused" — nothing refused, and NOT a survival claim (that is rc 5). Reported in
      # its own words so a reader is never told a verdict the actuator explicitly declined to make;
      # not counted as reaped, so the next sweep re-observes rather than this one guessing.
      say "    cc-teardown returned 6 (INDETERMINATE) — process gone, pane fate UNREADABLE; NOT a refusal and NOT a survivor claim; left for the next sweep to re-observe"
      log "teardown-indeterminate $name rc=6 pane=$pane"
    else
      say "    cc-teardown returned $trc (10=DEFER 2=REFUSE 5=FAIL) — its gate refused; NOT reaped (trusted)"; log "teardown-refused $name rc=$trc"
    fi
  done

  # ── RIDE-ALONG WORK — bounded, and OFF the decision path (R5, SESSION_REGISTRY_V2 §4.3.1) ───────
  # Neither of these feeds the reap decision: `cc-backlog reap` heals the CLAIM ledger and
  # `cc-inbox-guard sweep` escalates unconsumed mail. They ran BEFORE classify purely because that is
  # where they were added — which put two unbounded foreign forks between the sweep's start and the
  # evidence, and made the reaper the cron carrier for three subsystems' periodic work. They still
  # ride the reaper's cadence (that part was always the point), but they can no longer delay a
  # decision or the act that follows it. Both are append-only/idempotent and self-damping, so running
  # them after the loop changes nothing about what they do.
  # BOUND SIZED TO THE BAND (2026-08-25, recycle #223) — the SIBLING of the note below, fixed the
  # same way and for the same reason, one block later. #219 raised GUARD 60 -> 600 and ACQUITTED
  # this one in the same breath: the SIZING note above recorded "backlog-reap 42.9% ... tracking
  # load, i.e. behaving like detectors". That number is reproducible (44.3% all-time re-derived
  # here, predicate: fires / (fires + completions)) and the conclusion drawn from it is wrong,
  # because the acquittal was computed as a 12-day AGGREGATE while the conviction next to it was
  # computed PER DAY. Per day this bound does not track load — it is a monotone ratchet:
  #     08-13  17.9%   08-16   8.5%   08-19  51.1%   08-22  81.7%   08-25  100.0%
  #     08-14   8.5%   08-17  17.2%   08-20  54.7%   08-23  87.5%   (76 fires, 0 completions)
  #     08-15   6.7%   08-18  47.2%   08-21  64.2%   08-24  92.5%
  # Load oscillates; this climbs and saturates. The aggregate is the mean of that curve, and it is
  # the resolution difference — not the data — that separated the two forks.
  #
  # A/B, live 14.4k-line ledger copied to a mktemp store (CC_BACKLOG_FILE + CC_BACKLOG_IDL both
  # redirected; the live ledger's sha256 was identical before and after, so isolation is proven by
  # content), non-dry, BOTH arms clearing the IDENTICAL 11 rows and writing 12 verdict rows — one
  # input moved, the scheduling band:
  #     foreground                  PRI 31 (32/32 samples)     96s
  #     taskpolicy -c background    PRI  4 (78/78 samples)    617s   <- ProcessType Background
  # A 6.4x band tax, independently reproducing the 6.6x the GUARD note below measured on a
  # different subject. The READ-ONLY half alone (--dry-run) is 18s foreground and 41s in-band, so
  # 68% of a 60s budget was spent before the first write. In this band the reap affords ZERO
  # writes inside 60s, and a bound that cannot fit the read half of healthy work can only fire.
  #
  # WHAT IT COST, measured the same day: `cmd_reap` runs the off-box CURE SWEEP as its LAST stage,
  # so the truncation always lands there. Eleven blocked rows were each adjudicated curable by the
  # sweep's own dry run — 7 WOULD-UNBLOCK (workers STALLED 7-20h) and 4 WOULD-CLOSE (workers
  # LANDED, content present on origin/main) — while the newest completed cure sweep in the log was
  # 23.5h old and only three have EVER completed. Finished work sat blocked for a day because the
  # stage that releases it is the stage the bound always cuts.
  #
  # 900s is ~1.5x the measured healthy in-band cost, and that 617s was measured under a concurrent
  # trunk-wide bats run (load 18-21); at ordinary load the band ratio puts a healthy run nearer
  # 220s, so this is ~4x. Still a genuine WEDGE cut — the 5-day gate blockage came from an
  # UNBOUNDED fork and that must stay impossible — but far enough above healthy work to carry
  # information again. The same trade the block below already accepted, and for the same stated
  # reasons: this fork is off the decision path, runs last, is append-only/idempotent, and the
  # single-instance sweep lock SKIPs rather than queues.
  if [ "$reap" = 1 ] && [ -n "$BACKLOG_BIN" ]; then
    local bout brc
    bout="$(_rp_bounded "${CC_REAPER_BACKLOG_TIMEOUT_S:-900}" "$BACKLOG_BIN" reap 2>/dev/null)"; brc=$?
    # NAME THE STAGE HONESTLY. `cmd_reap` is claim sweep -> cure sweep, and the cut lands on
    # whichever was running — systematically the LAST one. Saying "claim-ledger sweep INCOMPLETE"
    # attributed 732 cuts to the stage that had usually already finished, so the log reported the
    # wrong subsystem every time (memory: wrong-cause-corroborated-by-true-metric).
    [ "$brc" = 124 ] && log "bound-fired backlog-reap: exceeded ${CC_REAPER_BACKLOG_TIMEOUT_S:-900}s — reap INCOMPLETE this cadence (claim sweep and/or the off-box cure sweep, which runs LAST and is therefore cut first)"
    # R5d (recycle #225) — the same one-directional guard as the reconcile fork above; see there.
    [ "$brc" != 0 ] && [ "$brc" != 124 ] && log "backlog-reap FAILED: rc=$brc — producer error, NOT the bound; the claim and cure sweeps did NOT run this cadence"
    [ -n "$bout" ] && { say "$bout"; log "backlog-reap: $bout"; }
  fi
  # BOUND SIZED TO THE BAND (2026-08-25, recycle #219). This default was 60s and fired on
  # 1,564 of 1,646 sweeps — 95.0% all-time, and 82-100% on EVERY ONE of the log's 12 days,
  # including the quietest (2026-08-15: classify fired 13%, backlog-reap 7%, reconcile 0%,
  # median sweep 119s — and inbox-guard still fired 88%). Its three sibling bounds swing an
  # order of magnitude with load; this one does not move, which is what says it is measuring
  # nothing. That is the exact failure the SIZING note at the top of this file forbids by
  # name: "a bound sized from a foreground run is a permanent non-verdict: it fires on
  # healthy work and proves nothing."
  # A/B, same isolated 872-box store copy, same non-dry sweep, both arms emitting the
  # IDENTICAL 362 escalations, ONE input moved — the scheduling band:
  #     foreground (PRI 31, 6/6 samples)                       38s
  #     taskpolicy -c background (PRI 4, 12/48 samples)       249s   ← what launchd gives us
  # 249s is a HEALTHY run in this job's own band, so a 60s bound could only ever fire. 600s
  # is ~2.4x that measured healthy cost: still a genuine wedge cut (the 5-day gate blockage
  # came from an UNBOUNDED fork, and that must stay impossible), but far enough above healthy
  # work to carry information again.
  # It costs sweep wall-clock, and that is the right trade here: this fork is off the decision
  # path, runs last, and is append-only/idempotent, while the single-instance lock SKIPs rather
  # than queues — so an overrunning sweep is already the tolerated case (46.2% of sweeps
  # exceed the 300s StartInterval today). What the 60s bound bought instead was a fail-loud
  # subsystem cut mid-worklist on every single sweep.
  if [ "$reap" = 1 ] && [ -n "$GUARD_BIN" ]; then
    local gout grc
    gout="$(_rp_bounded "${CC_REAPER_GUARD_TIMEOUT_S:-600}" "$GUARD_BIN" sweep 2>/dev/null)"; grc=$?
    [ "$grc" = 124 ] && log "bound-fired inbox-guard: exceeded ${CC_REAPER_GUARD_TIMEOUT_S:-600}s — mail escalation INCOMPLETE this cadence"
    # R5d (recycle #225) — the same one-directional guard again, and this is the fork where the arm is
    # actually REACHABLE: cc-inbox-guard exits 2 at its own :53/:95 dependency guards (jq, and the
    # mailbox lib it sources out of hooks/lib/). Until now that exit produced a sweep that was
    # byte-identical in this log to a healthy one — and BETTER-looking than a healthy one, because the
    # bound-fired line that normally accompanies this fork on 96% of sweeps would simply be absent.
    [ "$grc" != 0 ] && [ "$grc" != 124 ] && log "inbox-guard FAILED: rc=$grc — producer error, NOT the bound; mail escalation did NOT run this cadence"
    [ -n "$gout" ] && log "inbox-guard: $gout"
  fi

  # ── T-P3-3 re-arm: a pane no longer in the surface set (recovered / cause cleared) drops its damping
  #    marker, so a future re-hang re-pages. A persistent hang stays surfaced every sweep → never cleared.
  #    (Page-marker LIFECYCLE, the clear_page analog — NOT an evidence-horizon reaper; see reaper-horizon-lint.)
  if [ "$reap" = 1 ] && [ -d "$PAGEDIR" ]; then
    local mk bn
    for mk in "$PAGEDIR"/*.cause; do
      [ -e "$mk" ] || continue
      bn="$(basename "$mk" .cause)"
      case "$surfaced_keys" in *" $bn "*) ;; *) rm -f "$mk" 2>/dev/null || true ;; esac
    done
  fi

  say "cc-reaper: $n classified · $candidates safe candidate(s) · $reaped reaped · $surfaced surfaced"
  log "sweep end: $n classified $candidates candidates $reaped reaped $surfaced surfaced"

  # Gap-3: record this sweep's end wall-clock so the NEXT sweep can detect an inter-sweep suspend.
  # Gated on --reap (a DRY-RUN sweep writes nothing, preserving its contract) — the live launchd loop
  # always runs --reap, so inter-sweep detection is unaffected.
  if [ "$reap" = 1 ]; then
    mkdir -p "$(dirname "$BEAT_FILE")" 2>/dev/null || true
    printf '%s\n' "$(reaper_now)" > "$BEAT_FILE" 2>/dev/null || true
  fi

  # ── P0-12b: the blind-spot self-check runs once per sweep, AFTER classification (enum = what we saw). ──
  self_check "$reap" "$n"
  return 0
}

# ── DIALECT + UNREADABLE-INSTRUMENT (backlog 7a00b5de1ec0) ───────────────────────────────────────
# `ps -o lstart=` renders through LC_TIME *and* TZ, so ONE live pid reads as several different
# strings depending on who is looking. Measured on this box, same pid, same instant:
#   canonical (TZ=UTC LC_ALL=C)  `Fri Aug 21 15:45:00 2026`
#   ambient   (LANG=en_CA.UTF-8) `Fri 21 Aug 08:45:00 2026`
#   LC_ALL=C at local TZ         `Fri Aug 21 08:45:00 2026`
# A cc-reaper sweep started from a session writes one dialect; a sweep started by launchd (no LANG
# ⇒ C) reads another. The lock file is written HERE and read HERE, so this file owns both ends: the
# write is pinned canonical, and the read accepts every dialect the fleet is measured to produce so
# records already on disk are not called strangers (memory: control-must-replay-the-real-artifact).
#
# FAIL DIRECTION, and it is the opposite of land-inflight's: a mismatch here BREAKS the sweep lock,
# and a broken lock means two concurrent reapers — a leg that KILLS. So every unusable reading is
# honoured as "holder alive, skip this tick". Skipping a tick is free; overlapping sweeps are not.
# The prior line returned FALSE on an unreadable `ps`, i.e. it broke the mutex on a ps hiccup.
proc_lstart(){ TZ=UTC LC_ALL=C ps -o lstart= -p "$1" 2>/dev/null | tr -s ' ' | sed 's/^ *//;s/ *$//'; }

# 0 iff <recorded> is NOT evidence that <pid> is a stranger. Cannot manufacture a false match: every
# candidate re-renders the SAME pid at the SAME moment, so no rendering of a different start instant
# can equal the record — the time-of-day digits differ in every dialect.
lstart_matches(){ # <recorded> <pid>
  local want="${1:-}" pid="${2:-}" now
  want="$(printf '%s' "$want" | tr -s ' ' | sed 's/^ *//;s/ *$//')"
  [ -n "$want" ] || return 0
  now="$(proc_lstart "$pid")"
  [ -n "$now" ] || return 0                        # unreadable instrument → honour, do not reap
  [ "$now" = "$want" ] && return 0
  now="$(ps -o lstart= -p "$pid" 2>/dev/null | tr -s ' ' | sed 's/^ *//;s/ *$//')"
  [ -n "$now" ] && [ "$now" = "$want" ] && return 0            # migration: reader-ambient
  now="$(LC_ALL=C ps -o lstart= -p "$pid" 2>/dev/null | tr -s ' ' | sed 's/^ *//;s/ *$//')"
  [ -n "$now" ] && [ "$now" = "$want" ] && return 0            # migration: C at local TZ
  return 1
}

# 0 iff THAT EXACT process is still running. `kill -0` alone answers "some process holds this pid",
# which a recycled pid satisfies forever — the failure mode that turns a crash into permanent dormancy.
lock_holder_alive(){ # <pid> <lstart>
  local pid="${1:-}" want="${2:-}"
  [ -n "$pid" ] || return 1
  kill -0 "$pid" 2>/dev/null || return 1
  [ -n "$want" ] || return 0                       # no pin recorded → pid liveness is all we have
  lstart_matches "$want" "$pid"
}

# 0 = lock held by us (caller proceeds) · 1 = another sweep owns this tick (caller exits 0, no work).
acquire_sweep_lock(){
  local breaks=0 pid lstart age lmt lnow
  mkdir -p "$(dirname "$LOCKDIR")" 2>/dev/null || true
  while ! mkdir "$LOCKDIR" 2>/dev/null; do
    pid="$(cat "$LOCKDIR/pid" 2>/dev/null || true)"
    lstart="$(cat "$LOCKDIR/lstart" 2>/dev/null || true)"
    lmt="$(stat -f %m "$LOCKDIR" 2>/dev/null || stat -c %Y "$LOCKDIR" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)"
    lnow="$(date +%s 2>/dev/null || echo 0)"
    age=$(( lnow - lmt ))
    if [ -z "$pid" ] && [ "$age" -lt "$LOCK_STAMP_GRACE_S" ]; then
      say "cc-reaper: another sweep is starting — skipping this tick"
      log "sweep skip: lock held (stamp pending, age ${age}s)"; return 1
    fi
    if lock_holder_alive "$pid" "$lstart" && [ "$age" -lt "$LOCK_MAX_AGE_S" ]; then
      say "cc-reaper: sweep already running (pid $pid, ${age}s in) — skipping this tick"
      log "sweep skip: lock held by live pid $pid (age ${age}s)"; return 1
    fi
    breaks=$(( breaks + 1 ))
    # Never fall through to an UNLOCKED sweep: proceeding is what the overlap costs us. Skipping is free.
    [ "$breaks" -gt 3 ] && { log "sweep lock: contended past $breaks break(s) — skipping this tick"; return 1; }
    log "sweep lock: stale (pid=${pid:-none} age=${age}s) — breaking"
    rm -rf "$LOCKDIR" 2>/dev/null || true
  done
  printf '%s\n' "$$" > "$LOCKDIR/pid" 2>/dev/null || true
  proc_lstart "$$" > "$LOCKDIR/lstart" 2>/dev/null || true
  # shellcheck disable=SC2064  # expand now: the trap must remove THIS sweep's dir, not a re-pointed var
  trap "rm -rf '$LOCKDIR' 2>/dev/null || true" EXIT INT TERM HUP
  return 0
}

# ── watchdog census (M4) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CENSUS ONLY. This leg never kills anything, by design and not by omission: a lead-crash-watchdog
# daemon is the one process on this machine whose entire job is to OUTLIVE its session, so an
# actuator that reaped it automatically would be able to disarm a live session's only crash handler.
# It classifies, reconciles against the daemon's own log, and PRINTS the exact command per orphan.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS: the daemon's liveness test was a bare `kill -0` on the captured lead pid. macOS
# recycles pids, so once the lead died and its pid was reused the test read ALIVE forever and neither
# of the poll loop's exit conditions could ever become true again. Measured 2026-07-29 before the
# fix: 63 live daemons, 14 of them named by no .daemon file, oldest 1d15h, against 157 spawns and
# ZERO logged exits of EITHER kind that day. hooks/lead-crash-watchdog.sh now pins {pid, lstart} and
# self-retires when superseded, which stops NEW daemons wedging; this census is how the already-wedged
# population is seen, and how we tell afterwards whether it is converging.
#
# Kill switch: CC_WATCHDOG_CENSUS=off
WD_CENSUS_DIR="${CC_WATCHDOG_DIR:-$HOME/.claude/watchdog}"
WD_CENSUS_LOG="${CC_WATCHDOG_LOG:-$HOME/.claude/logs/lead-crash-watchdog.log}"

wd_lstart(){ ps -o lstart= -p "${1:-0}" 2>/dev/null | tr -s ' ' | sed 's/^ *//;s/ *$//'; }

# 0 iff the pid is live AND is a claude binary. The command check is the whole point: a dead lead's
# pid gets RECYCLED, and answering "is this session alive" with bare pid liveness is the exact defect
# this census reports on — it must not commit that defect inside its own classifier.
wd_lead_live(){
  local p="${1:-}" c
  case "$p" in ''|*[!0-9]*) return 1 ;; esac
  kill -0 "$p" 2>/dev/null || return 1
  c="$(ps -o command= -p "$p" 2>/dev/null)"
  case "$c" in *claude.exe*|*node_modules/.bin/claude*) return 0 ;; esac
  return 1
}

# 0 iff the .daemon record still names the process it recorded. Deliberately {pid, lstart} with NO
# command check: this mirrors the watchdog's own daemon_alive predicate verbatim, so the census
# answers the same question production asks — and the positive control below can therefore exercise
# the real predicate instead of a lookalike. Unverifiable identity ⇒ not provably ours ⇒ not alive
# (same bias as daemon_alive, which re-spawns rather than trust an unpinned pid).
#
# DIALECT (backlog 7a00b5de1ec0): the `.daemon` record is written by ANOTHER PROGRAM —
# hooks/lead-crash-watchdog.sh — and read here, so the two ends can sit in different locales (a
# session writes `Fri 21 Aug 08:45:00 2026`; a launchd-started reaper reads `Fri Aug 21 08:45:00
# 2026` for the SAME live pid). An exact compare then calls every daemon a stranger. Read-side only:
# the write belongs to the watchdog, and re-pinning a shared record's format from the reading end is
# how a fix commits the bug it removes.
# 0 iff <recorded> is a rendering of <pid>'s OWN start instant, in any dialect this fleet is measured
# to produce. Cannot manufacture a false match: every candidate re-renders the SAME pid at the SAME
# moment, so a record of a DIFFERENT start instant matches none of them — the time-of-day digits
# differ in every dialect. Strict on an unreadable `ps` (rc 1), preserving wd_daemon_live's
# documented "unverifiable identity ⇒ not provably ours" bias; do NOT relax that here.
wd_lstart_matches(){ # <recorded> <pid>
  local want="${1:-}" pid="${2:-}" cur
  want="$(printf '%s' "$want" | tr -s ' ' | sed 's/^ *//;s/ *$//')"
  [ -n "$want" ] || return 1
  cur="$(TZ=UTC LC_ALL=C ps -o lstart= -p "$pid" 2>/dev/null | tr -s ' ' | sed 's/^ *//;s/ *$//')"
  [ -n "$cur" ] && [ "$cur" = "$want" ] && return 0
  cur="$(wd_lstart "$pid")"
  [ -n "$cur" ] && [ "$cur" = "$want" ] && return 0             # migration: reader-ambient
  cur="$(LC_ALL=C ps -o lstart= -p "$pid" 2>/dev/null | tr -s ' ' | sed 's/^ *//;s/ *$//')"
  [ -n "$cur" ] && [ "$cur" = "$want" ] && return 0             # migration: C at local TZ
  return 1
}

wd_daemon_live(){
  local pid="${1:-}" want="${2:-}"
  case "$pid" in ''|*[!0-9]*) return 1 ;; esac
  kill -0 "$pid" 2>/dev/null || return 1
  [ -n "$want" ] || return 1
  wd_lstart_matches "$want" "$pid"
}

# wd_classify <watchdog-dir> <sid> → CLASS<TAB>LEAD_PID<TAB>DAEMON_PID<TAB>DAEMON_LSTART
# The three classes are MECE over one sid and keyed on what is RUNNING, never on file age:
#   live-session : the recorded lead pid is a live claude → a legitimate watch, leave it alone
#   orphan       : the lead is gone or recycled but a daemon is still provably running → the wedge
#   stale-file   : neither lead nor daemon alive → filesystem residue, nothing to reap
wd_classify(){
  local dir="$1" sid="$2" lead="" dpid="" dstart=""
  lead="$(cat "$dir/$sid.pid" 2>/dev/null || true)"
  if [ -f "$dir/$sid.daemon" ]; then
    IFS=$'\t' read -r dpid dstart < "$dir/$sid.daemon" 2>/dev/null || true
  fi
  if wd_lead_live "$lead"; then
    printf 'live-session\t%s\t%s\t%s' "$lead" "$dpid" "$dstart"
  elif wd_daemon_live "$dpid" "$dstart"; then
    printf 'orphan\t%s\t%s\t%s' "$lead" "$dpid" "$dstart"
  else
    printf 'stale-file\t%s\t%s\t%s' "$lead" "$dpid" "$dstart"
  fi
}

# POSITIVE CONTROL (R6). A census whose classifier silently answered "live-session" for everything
# would print a clean, reassuring, entirely false report — and nothing in a normal run could tell the
# difference. So every run proves the orphan predicate FIRST, on a fixture built in a scratch dir:
# a lead pid that cannot exist (2147483647 > the 99999 macOS pid ceiling, so it is ESRCH by
# construction and can never be recycled into a live process under us) plus a daemon record naming a
# process that IS provably alive with its REAL lstart — this shell. That is exactly the orphan shape
# (lead gone, daemon running), and it drives the same wd_classify the real rows go through.
# Fork-free on purpose: a `sleep &` fixture would need reaping and prints stray job noise.
wd_control(){
  local d rc=1 out
  d="$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/wd-control.XXXXXX" 2>/dev/null)" || { printf 'control=FAIL reason=no-tmpdir'; return 1; }
  printf '2147483647\n' > "$d/ctl.pid"
  printf '%s\t%s\n' "$$" "$(wd_lstart "$$")" > "$d/ctl.daemon"
  out="$(wd_classify "$d" ctl | cut -f1)"
  [ "$out" = "orphan" ] && rc=0
  rm -rf "$d" 2>/dev/null || true
  if [ "$rc" = 0 ]; then printf 'control=OK (fixture dead-lead + live-pinned-daemon classified orphan)'
  else printf 'control=FAIL (fixture orphan classified as %s — the classifier is not deciding; every count below is UNTRUSTWORTHY)' "${out:-<empty>}"; fi
  return "$rc"
}

# wd_daemon_pids — the live daemon population, read from the PROCESS TABLE.
#
# THE ENUMERATION IS PROCESS-FIRST, NOT PIDFILE-FIRST, and that ordering is the whole design. A
# census keyed on ~/.claude/watchdog/*.pid can only ever see daemons that were successfully RECORDED
# — and the dominant leak class is precisely the daemons that were not (14 of 63 when measured,
# oldest 1d15h, because the parent hook was killed before it could write .daemon). Keyed on files,
# this leg would have reported a clean zero while those 14 ran: an actuator that cannot see its
# target population reports success by construction. So the process table is the source of truth and
# the pidfiles are what it is reconciled AGAINST.
#
# Matched on the script token inside ARGS, never `comm` (truncated to 16 chars, so every one of these
# reads "lead-crash-watch" and no exact match is possible) and never `pgrep -f` (which also matches
# any process whose argv merely MENTIONS the path — agent briefs quoting a filename have produced
# exactly that false census before). Anchored to a whitespace boundary and end-of-token so a path
# ending in the script name matches while a longer name does not.
#
# ONE ps call for the whole census, carrying {pid, lstart, etime} so the classification pass never
# forks again. The naive shape — enumerate pids, then `ps -o lstart=` per .daemon record per daemon —
# is O(daemons × records): 60 × 49 = ~2,900 forks on the machine this was written for, which turns a
# diagnostic into its own load event.
#
# lstart occupies exactly 5 whitespace-separated fields ("Wed 29 Jul 22:34:14 2026"), so args begin at
# field 8, and joining fields 2-6 with single spaces reproduces byte-for-byte what the watchdog's own
# `ps -o lstart= | tr -s ' '` wrote into the .daemon file — the two must match textually or every
# record reads stale.
#
# Seam: CC_WATCHDOG_PS_BIN — lets the positive control drive a synthetic process table.
wd_daemon_table(){
  "${CC_WATCHDOG_PS_BIN:-ps}" -axo pid,lstart,etime,args 2>/dev/null \
    | awk '{ for (i = 8; i <= 9 && i <= NF; i++)
               if ($i ~ /(^|\/)lead-crash-watchdog\.sh$/) {
                 printf "%s\t%s %s %s %s %s\t%s\n", $1, $2, $3, $4, $5, $6, $7; break } }' \
    | sort -u
}
wd_daemon_pids(){ wd_daemon_table | cut -f1; }

# SECOND POSITIVE CONTROL — the UNTRACKED leg (the one that matters most, and the one a file-keyed
# census could not have had at all). wd_control above proves the sid-keyed classifier decides; it
# says nothing about whether the process-table enumeration can SEE a daemon nobody recorded. Here a
# stub `ps` emits a synthetic table containing one daemon pid that appears in no .daemon file, and
# the enumerator must return it. If wd_daemon_pids' args-matching ever silently stops matching — a
# `comm` truncation, a changed ps format, an over-anchored pattern — the live census would quietly
# report zero orphans, which is exactly what "everything is fine" looks like.
wd_control_untracked(){
  local d stub out
  d="$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/wd-ctl2.XXXXXX" 2>/dev/null)" || { printf 'control-untracked=FAIL reason=no-tmpdir'; return 1; }
  stub="$d/ps"
  {
    printf '#!/bin/bash\n'
    printf 'printf "%%s\\n" "  PID STARTED                     ELAPSED ARGS"\n'
    printf 'printf "%%s\\n" "424242 Wed 29 Jul 22:34:14 2026 01-02:03:04 /bin/bash /w/hooks/lead-crash-watchdog.sh"\n'
    printf 'printf "%%s\\n" "424243 Wed 29 Jul 22:34:14 2026 01-02:03:04 /bin/bash /w/hooks/not-the-watchdog.sh"\n'
    printf 'printf "%%s\\n" "424244 Wed 29 Jul 22:34:14 2026 01-02:03:04 /w/claude --print fix hooks/lead-crash-watchdog.sh"\n'
  } > "$stub"
  chmod +x "$stub" 2>/dev/null || true
  out="$(CC_WATCHDOG_PS_BIN="$stub" wd_daemon_pids)"
  rm -rf "$d" 2>/dev/null || true
  if [ "$out" = "424242" ]; then
    printf 'control-untracked=OK (synthetic unrecorded daemon enumerated from the process table; both decoys ignored)'
    return 0
  fi
  printf 'control-untracked=FAIL (enumerator returned "%s", expected 424242 — the census CANNOT SEE untracked daemons; untracked_orphan=0 below means nothing)' "${out:-<empty>}"
  return 1
}

watchdog_census(){
  local json=0; [ "${1:-}" = "--json" ] && json=1
  if [ "${CC_WATCHDOG_CENSUS:-on}" = "off" ]; then
    say "cc-reaper watchdog-census: DISABLED (CC_WATCHDOG_CENSUS=off) — no census taken"; return 0
  fi

  local ctl ctl_rc=0 ctl2 ctl2_rc=0
  ctl="$(wd_control)" || ctl_rc=1
  ctl2="$(wd_control_untracked)" || ctl2_rc=1

  # PASS 1 — every live daemon process, classified by whether a .daemon record still names it and by
  # whether the lead that record points at is really alive.
  # Every .daemon record read ONCE, as pid<TAB>lstart<TAB>sid, so the join below is in-memory.
  local records="" f rp rl
  for f in "$WD_CENSUS_DIR"/*.daemon; do
    [ -f "$f" ] || continue
    IFS=$'\t' read -r rp rl < "$f" 2>/dev/null || true
    [ -n "$rp" ] || continue
    records="${records}${rp}"$'\t'"${rl}"$'\t'"$(basename "$f" .daemon)"$'\n'
  done

  local n_tlive=0 n_torph=0 n_untracked=0 n_stale=0 rows="" jrows="" p sid dstart lead et seen="" plstart rec
  while IFS=$'\t' read -r p plstart et; do
    [ -n "$p" ] || continue
    # Which sid, if any, records THIS process? Matched on {pid, lstart} against the records read
    # above — a record naming a RECYCLED pid is not a record of this process, and trusting the pid
    # alone here would reproduce, inside the census, the exact defect the census exists to report.
    sid=""; dstart=""
    rec="$(printf '%s' "$records" | awk -F'\t' -v P="$p" -v L="$plstart" '$1==P && $2==L {print $3; exit}')"
    # DIALECT FALLBACK (backlog 7a00b5de1ec0). The exact join above compares two INDEPENDENTLY
    # rendered strings: `plstart` comes from this reaper's own bulk `ps` (its ambient locale/TZ),
    # while the record was written by hooks/lead-crash-watchdog.sh in ITS ambient. Same locale ⇒
    # they match and this fallback never runs. Different locale — a launchd-started reaper reading a
    # session-written record — ⇒ the join misses for EVERY daemon, each one is then classed
    # UNTRACKED, and the census prints `▶ kill` for a live, correctly-tracked watchdog.
    #
    # Exact-first is deliberate: pinning the bulk `ps` to a canonical dialect instead would stop
    # today's ambient-written records from matching, i.e. it would commit the bug it removes. The
    # fork cost stays bounded by the number of UNEXPLAINED daemons (normally zero), never one fork
    # per daemon, so the single-ps design this census was built around is preserved.
    if [ -z "$rec" ]; then
      local crp crl crs
      while IFS=$'\t' read -r crp crl crs; do
        [ "$crp" = "$p" ] || continue
        if wd_lstart_matches "$crl" "$p"; then rec="$crs"; plstart="$crl"; break; fi
      done <<< "$records"
    fi
    if [ -n "$rec" ]; then sid="$rec"; dstart="$plstart"; fi
    if [ -z "$sid" ]; then
      n_untracked=$(( n_untracked + 1 ))
      rows="${rows}  ▶ kill ${p}  # UNTRACKED watchdog daemon, age ${et:-?} — named by no .daemon record"$'\n'
      jrows="${jrows}{\"sid\":null,\"class\":\"UNTRACKED-orphan\",\"daemon_pid\":\"$p\",\"age\":\"${et:-?}\"},"
      continue
    fi
    seen="$seen $sid"
    lead="$(cat "$WD_CENSUS_DIR/$sid.pid" 2>/dev/null || true)"
    if wd_lead_live "$lead"; then
      n_tlive=$(( n_tlive + 1 ))
      jrows="${jrows}{\"sid\":\"$sid\",\"class\":\"tracked-live\",\"lead_pid\":\"$lead\",\"daemon_pid\":\"$p\"},"
    else
      n_torph=$(( n_torph + 1 ))
      rows="${rows}  ▶ kill ${p}  # watchdog for dead session ${sid}, lstart ${dstart}"$'\n'
      jrows="${jrows}{\"sid\":\"$sid\",\"class\":\"tracked-orphan\",\"lead_pid\":\"$lead\",\"daemon_pid\":\"$p\"},"
    fi
  done <<< "$(wd_daemon_table)"

  # PASS 2 — reconcile the OTHER direction: pidfiles with no live daemon and no live lead. Pure
  # filesystem residue; nothing to kill, but it is what the sid-keyed classifier (and its positive
  # control) decides, so it is counted rather than assumed empty.
  # Both of wd_classify's remaining verdicts are consumed here, not just the tidy one. A pass that
  # counted stale-file and silently dropped orphan would make the class the census exists to surface
  # disappear whenever the process-table scan had not already caught it — a third state skipping the
  # gate that was supposed to be MECE with it.
  local cls crow
  for f in "$WD_CENSUS_DIR"/*.pid; do
    [ -f "$f" ] || continue
    sid="$(basename "$f" .pid)"
    case " $seen " in *" $sid "*) continue ;; esac
    crow="$(wd_classify "$WD_CENSUS_DIR" "$sid")"
    cls="$(printf '%s' "$crow" | cut -f1)"
    lead="$(printf '%s' "$crow" | cut -f2)"
    p="$(printf '%s' "$crow" | cut -f3)"
    dstart="$(printf '%s' "$crow" | cut -f4)"
    # Same field set as pass 1's rows: a consumer should not have to know WHICH pass produced a row
    # to know whether lead_pid will be there.
    case "$cls" in
      orphan)     n_torph=$(( n_torph + 1 ))
                  rows="${rows}  ▶ kill ${p}  # watchdog for dead session ${sid}, lstart ${dstart}"$'\n'
                  jrows="${jrows}{\"sid\":\"$sid\",\"class\":\"tracked-orphan\",\"lead_pid\":\"$lead\",\"daemon_pid\":\"$p\"}," ;;
      stale-file) n_stale=$(( n_stale + 1 ))
                  jrows="${jrows}{\"sid\":\"$sid\",\"class\":\"stale-file\",\"lead_pid\":\"$lead\"}," ;;
      live-session) n_tlive=$(( n_tlive + 1 ))
                  jrows="${jrows}{\"sid\":\"$sid\",\"class\":\"tracked-live\",\"lead_pid\":\"$lead\"}," ;;
    esac
  done

  # Reconcile against the daemon's OWN log. spawned − (clean-exit + superseded + crash-path) is the
  # accounting identity for "should still be running"; the delta against the observed live count is
  # the leak. A zero-exit day with a positive spawn count is the wedge signature, and it is only
  # visible as a RATIO — either number alone looks unremarkable.
  local n_spawn=0 n_gone=0 n_sup=0 n_crash=0 n_dedupe=0 n_retire=0 n_procs=0 expect delta
  if [ -r "$WD_CENSUS_LOG" ]; then
    # NO `|| echo 0` on any of these: `grep -c` ALREADY prints 0 on no-match and exits 1, so the
    # fallback would append a SECOND line and the count becomes the two-line string "0\n0", which
    # blows up the $(( )) below. This script runs without errexit, so a rc-1 grep in a command
    # substitution is already safe and captures the "0" grep itself printed.
    n_spawn=$(grep -cF 'spawned watchdog daemon' "$WD_CENSUS_LOG" 2>/dev/null)
    n_gone=$(grep -cF 'pid file gone — exit' "$WD_CENSUS_LOG" 2>/dev/null)
    n_sup=$(grep -cF 'SUPERSEDED — pidfile holds' "$WD_CENSUS_LOG" 2>/dev/null)
    n_crash=$(grep -cF 'LEAD CRASH detected' "$WD_CENSUS_LOG" 2>/dev/null)
    n_dedupe=$(grep -cF 'ALREADY RUNNING' "$WD_CENSUS_LOG" 2>/dev/null)
    n_retire=$(grep -cF 'retired stale watchdog' "$WD_CENSUS_LOG" 2>/dev/null)
  fi
  # Same enumerator the classification pass used — never a second, differently-written scan, or the
  # reconcile could disagree with the rows it is reconciling.
  n_procs=$(wd_daemon_pids | grep -c .)
  expect=$(( n_spawn - n_gone - n_sup - n_crash ))
  delta=$(( n_procs - expect ))

  if [ "$json" = 1 ]; then
    printf '{"control_ok":%s,"control_untracked_ok":%s,' \
      "$( [ "$ctl_rc" = 0 ] && printf true || printf false )" \
      "$( [ "$ctl2_rc" = 0 ] && printf true || printf false )"
    printf '"counts":{"tracked_live":%s,"tracked_orphan":%s,"untracked_orphan":%s,"stale_file":%s,"live_procs":%s},' \
      "$n_tlive" "$n_torph" "$n_untracked" "$n_stale" "$n_procs"
    printf '"log":{"spawned":%s,"clean_exit":%s,"superseded":%s,"crash_path":%s,"dedupe_skips":%s,"retired":%s,"expected_running":%s,"delta":%s},' \
      "$n_spawn" "$n_gone" "$n_sup" "$n_crash" "$n_dedupe" "$n_retire" "$expect" "$delta"
    printf '"rows":[%s]}\n' "${jrows%,}"
  else
    say "cc-reaper watchdog-census — CENSUS ONLY, nothing was killed"
    say "  $ctl"
    say "  $ctl2"
    say "  daemon procs : live=$n_procs  →  tracked-live=$n_tlive  tracked-orphan=$n_torph  UNTRACKED-orphan=$n_untracked"
    say "  pidfile-only : stale-file=$n_stale (no live daemon, no live lead — residue)"
    say "  log reconcile: spawned=$n_spawn  clean-exit=$n_gone  superseded=$n_sup  crash-path=$n_crash"
    say "                 dedupe-skips=$n_dedupe  retired-stale=$n_retire"
    say "                 expected-still-running=$expect  observed=$n_procs  delta=$delta"
    if [ -n "$rows" ]; then
      say "  reap commands (run these yourself — this leg never kills):"
      printf '%s' "$rows"
    else
      say "  no orphans — nothing to reap"
    fi
  fi
  return 0
}

case "${1:-}" in
  # Only --reap is serialized. A DRY-RUN neither takes the lock nor respects it: it writes nothing
  # (reconcile / backlog-reap / inbox-guard are all --reap-gated), so it has no state to race on, and
  # gating it was wrong in BOTH directions — a human running the diagnostic could suppress a real reap
  # tick for the duration, and the diagnostic became unavailable during exactly the long sweep that
  # made someone want to run it. Its ~12s of read-only git I/O is not worth either.
  sweep)        [ "${2:-}" = "--reap" ] && { acquire_sweep_lock || exit 0; }
                sweep "${2:-}" ;;
  # CENSUS ONLY and therefore unlocked: it kills nothing and writes nothing, so it has no state to
  # race a sweep on, and it must stay available during exactly the long sweep that made someone ask.
  watchdog-census|--watchdog-census) watchdog_census "${2:-}" ;;
  # The garbage arm standalone — DRY-RUN by default, --reap to act. Unlocked like the census: its
  # candidate set (orphans of DEAD parents, stuck wrappers, dead-lead watchdogs) is disjoint from
  # what a concurrent sweep classifies, its kills are per-pid re-verified, and it is exactly the
  # command an operator wants DURING the overload a locked sweep is stuck in.
  garbage)      if [ "${2:-}" = "--reap" ]; then garbage_sweep 1; else garbage_sweep 0; fi ;;
  selftest)     exec bats "$HERE/../tests/cc-reaper.bats" ;;
  # 2,29 — the command list plus the KILL SWITCH block, ending on its closing blank comment. The
  # window has been bumped by every header addition (2,25 → 2,30 for the garbage entry, before
  # that for watchdog-census); this one re-anchors it on a real boundary instead, so the next
  # addition truncates the SURFACE PAGING prose that follows rather than the switch a reader came
  # here to find. Help is where an operator LEARNS the reaper can be stopped — it goes above the
  # design notes, not below them.
  -h|--help|"") sed -n '2,29p' "$0"; exit 0 ;;
  *)            echo "cc-reaper: unknown command '$1' (sweep | garbage | watchdog-census | selftest)" >&2; exit 2 ;;
esac
